<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch3-circuittheorems">
	<title>Circuit Theorems</title>

	<introduction>
	
		<p>In this chapter, various theorems that can be used to analyze circuit properties will be explored. These theorems are all handy tools that can be placed into our circuit analysis toolbox. Between this and the previous chapter, all of the tools and skills required to solve all of the problems in this textbook will have been made available. The remaining chapters in this book will use these tools to analyze interesting and complex circuits.</p>

		<p>The theorems presented in this chapter apply to <term>linear circuits</term>. A linear function behaves in such a manner that the property given in <xref ref="ch3linearity" /> applies.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch3linearity">F(at_1 + bt_2) = aF(t_1) + bF(t_2)</md>
		</p>

		<example>
			<title>Identifying linear functions</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Identify which (if any) of the following functions are linear.</p>

				<p>
					<ol>
						<li><p><m>f(t) = 2t</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>f(t) = 3t+5</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>f(t) = t^2</m></p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>

				<p>For each of the three functions, check for linearity by seeing if <m>f(at_1 + bt_2) = af(t_1) + bf(t_2)</m>.</p>

				<p>Function 1 is linear:</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f(at_1 + bt_2) \amp= </mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 2(at_1 + bt_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 2at_1 + 2bt_2</mrow>
						<mrow>af(t_1) + bf(t_2) \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= a2t_1 + b2t_2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Function 2 is not linear:</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f(at_1 + bt_2) \amp= </mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 3(at_1 + bt_2) + 5</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 3at_1 + 3bt_2 + 5</mrow>
						<mrow>af(t_1) + bf(t_2) \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= a(3t_1 + 5) + b(3t_2 + 5)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= a3t_1 + b3t_2 + 5(a+b)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Function 3 is not linear:</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f(at_1 + bt_2) \amp= </mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= (at_1 + bt_2)^2</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= a^2t_1^2 + abt_1t_2 + b^2t_2^2</mrow>
						<mrow>af(t_1) + bf(t_2) \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= at_1^2 + bt_2^2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<p>Mathematical operators are said to be linear if they similarly obey <xref ref="ch3linearity" />. <xref ref="ch3examplelinearoperators" /> will prove that differentiation and integration are linear operators. This is why circuits containing capacitors and inductors are linear circuits, even though they are described by differentiation and/or integration operations.</p>

		<example xml:id="ch3examplelinearoperators">
			<title>Identifying linear operators</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Identify which (if any) of the following operators are linear.</p>

				<p>
					<ol>
						<li><p><m>\ln</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>\frac{d}{dt}</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>\int dt</m></p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>

				<p>Operator 1 is not linear:</p>

				<p>
					<md>\ln(f(at_1 + bt_2)) \neq a\ln(f(t_1)) + b\ln(f(t_2))</md>
				</p>

				<p>Verify by applying to a linear function <m>f(t) = 2t</m>. Let <m>a = 5</m> and <m>b = 2</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\ln(f(5t_1 + 2t_2)) \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \ln(2(5t_1+2t_2))</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \ln(10t_1 + 4t_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>5\ln(f(t_1)) + 2\ln(f(t_2))\amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5\ln(2t_1) + 2\ln(2t_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \ln((2t_1)^5) + \ln((2t_2)^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \ln(((2t_1)^5)(2t_2)^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \ln((32t_1^5)(4t_2^2))</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \ln(128t_1^5t_2^2)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Operator 2 is linear:</p>

				<p>
					<md>\frac{d}{dt}f(at_1 + bt_2) = a\frac{d}{dt}f(t_1) + b\frac{d}{dt}f(t_2)</md>
				</p>

				<p>Verify by applying to a linear function <m>f(t) = 2t</m>. Let <m>a = 5</m> and <m>b = 2</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}f(5t_1 + 2t_2) \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{d}{dt}f(5t_1 + 2t_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{d}{dt}[2(5t_1 + 2t_2)]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{d}{dt}(10t_1 + 4t_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{d}{dt}(10t_1) + \frac{d}{dt}(4t_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 10\frac{d}{dt}t_1 + 4\frac{d}{dt}t_2</mrow>
						<mrow>5\frac{d}{dt}f(t_1) + 2\frac{d}{dt}f(t_2) \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5\frac{d}{dt}(2t_1) + 2\frac{d}{dt}(2t_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 10\frac{d}{dt}t_1 + 4\frac{d}{dt}t_2</mrow>
					</md>
					
				</p>

				<p>Operator 3 is linear:</p>

				<p>
					<md>\int f(at_1 + bt_2)\,dt = a\int f(t_1)\,dt + b\int f(t_2)\,dt</md>
				</p>

				<p>Verify by applying to a linear function <m>f(t) = 2t</m>. Let <m>a = 5</m> and <m>b = 2</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\int f(5t_1 + 2t_2)\,dt \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \int 2(5t_1 + 2t_2)\,dt</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \int (10t_1 + 4t_2)\,dt</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \int (10t_1)\,dt + \int (4t_2)\,dt</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 10 \int t_1\,dt + 4 \int t_2\,dt</mrow>
						<mrow>5\int f(t_1)\,dt + 2\int f(t_2)\,dt \amp=</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5\int 2t_1\,dt + 2 \int 2t_2\,dt</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 10\int t_1\,dt + 4 \int t_2\,dt</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>
	
	</introduction>

	<section xml:id="ch3-superposition">
		<title>Superposition</title>

		<p>Superposition applies to all linear circuits (in fact, <xref ref="ch3linearity" /> describes the property of superposition, which is obeyed in linear systems). If a linear circuit has <m>n</m> independent sources, then <m>n</m> subcircuits can be created, each with one of the independent sources activated and all others deactivated. Add the properties of each of the subcircuits together to find the total value of the property. To deactivate a voltage source, replace the voltage source with a short circuit (corresponding to 0<nbsp />V). To deactivate a current source, replace the current source with an open circuit (corresponding to 0<nbsp />A). Any dependent sources that exist in the circuit cannot be eliminated and must be present in all subcircuits.</p>

		<p>For example, if measuring a branch current <m>I_X</m> in a circuit with three independent sources, calculate <m>I_{X1}</m> with the first source active and the other two deactivated; then calculate <m>I_{X2}</m> with the second source active and the other two deactivated; finally, calculate <m>I_{X3}</m> with the third source active and the other two deactivated. Then, <m>I_X = I_{X1} + I_{X2} + I_{X3}</m>. The method used to calculate each individual branch current can be from any method (Ohm's law, voltage divider, current divider, KCL/KVL, or mesh analysis).</p>

		<p>It is not necessary to perform superposition to determine circuit properties. As discussed in the previous chapter, KCL/KVL and mesh analysis are capable of solving for any unknown current or voltage in a circuit. However, superposition may be simpler and more straightforward to solve, especially in cases with few independent sources.</p>

		<example>
			<title>Superposition in symbolic form</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>I</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="superpositionExampleFigure" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionExampleFigure">
  					<caption>Circuit schematic used to demonstrate the superposition theorem.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionExampleFigure">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a current source Is, voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node A. Is flows into node A. R2 and the low-potential end of Vs connect at node B. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of Vs connect at node C. Is flows out of node C. Branch current I is defined through R1 and flows out of node A into node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$] (-2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (-2.5,2.5) to [short] (0,2.5);
								\draw[] (0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, i^=$I$] (0,0);
								\draw[] (0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (2.5,0) to [V, l_=$V_S$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [short] (2.5,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>As there are two independent sources, two subcircuits can be created. The first, shown in <xref ref="superpositionSubcircuitFigure1" /> contains only the current source. The voltage source is deactivated (replaced with a short circuit).</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionSubcircuitFigure1">
  					<caption>A subcircuit of <xref ref="superpositionExampleFigure" /> with the voltage source deactivated.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionSubcircuitFigure1">
    					<shortdescription>Same circuit as above with the voltage source shorted out.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$] (-2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (-2.5,2.5) to [short] (0,2.5);
								\draw[] (0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, i^=$I_1$] (0,0);
								\draw[] (0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (2.5,0) to [short] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [short] (2.5,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>There are many tools that can be used to calculate <m>I_1</m>. This example will utilize the current divider rule.</p>

				<p>
					<md>I_1 = I_S	\left(	\frac{R_1//R_2}{R_1}\right)</md>
				</p>

				<p>The second subcircuit, shown in <xref ref="superpositionSubcircuitFigure2" />, contains the voltage source. The current source has been deactivated by replacing it with an open circuit.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionSubcircuitFigure2">
  					<caption>A subcircuit of <xref ref="superpositionExampleFigure" /> with the current source deactivated.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionSubcircuitFigure2">
    					<shortdescription>Same circuit as above with the current source replaced with an open circuit.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [open] (-2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (-2.5,2.5) to [short] (0,2.5);
								\draw[] (0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, i^=$I_2$] (0,0);
								\draw[] (0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (2.5,0) to [V, l_=$V_S$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [short] (2.5,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_2</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>I_2 = -\frac{V_S}{R_1+R_2}</md>
				</p>

				<p>The total current, <m>I</m>, through the original circuit shown in <xref ref="superpositionExampleFigure" /> is equal to the sum of both of the subcircuit currents.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>I \amp= I_1 + I_2</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= I_S \left(	\frac{R_1//R_2}{R_1}\right) - \frac{V_S}{R_1+R_2}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<example>
			<title>Superposition in numeric form</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure1" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure1">
  					<caption>A circuit with two independent sources used to demonstrate superposition.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure1">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 5V source, 10mA source, 1.8k resistor, 2.4k resistor, 5.1k resistor, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor. The 1.8k and 2.4k resistors are connected at node a. The 2.4k resistor, 10mA source, 5.1k resistor, and 3.3k resistors are connected at node b. The 10mA source flows out of node b. The 5.1k resistor and high-potential end of the 5V source are connected at node c. The 10mA source, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor are connected at node d. The 10mA source flows into node d. The 1.8k resistor, low-potential end of the 5V source, and 5.6k resistors are connected at node e. Voltage "Vout" drops over the 5.6k resistor between nodes d and e.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$] (0,0);
								\draw (0,5) to [R, l^=2.4~k$\Omega$] (2.5,5);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] (5,0);
								\draw (2.5,5) to [R, l_=5.1~k$\Omega$, *-] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V, -*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,5) to [R, l^=3.3~k$\Omega$] (5,5);
								\draw (5,5) to [R, l_=5.6~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_{OUT}$, *-] (5,0);
								\draw (2.5,6.5) to [I, l^=10~mA] (5,6.5);
								\draw (2.5,5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
								\draw (5,5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>First, deactivate the current source (replace it with an open circuit) and calculate <m>V_{OUT,1}</m>, the contribution to <m>V_{OUT}</m> from the voltage source. This circuit is shown in <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure2" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure2">
  					<caption>The subcircuit of <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure1" /> with the current source deactivated.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure2">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 5V source, 1.8k resistor, 2.4k resistor, 5.1k resistor, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor. The 1.8k and 2.4k resistors are connected at node a. The 2.4k resistor, 5.1k resistor, and 3.3k resistors are connected at node b. The 5.1k resistor and high-potential end of the 5V source are connected at node c. The 3.3k resistor and 5.6k resistor are connected at node d. The 1.8k resistor, low-potential end of the 5V source, and 5.6k resistors are connected at node e. Voltage "Vout,1" drops over the 5.6k resistor between nodes d and e.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$] (0,0);
								\draw (0,5) to [R, l^=2.4~k$\Omega$] (2.5,5);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] (5,0);
								\draw (2.5,5) to [R, l_=5.1~k$\Omega$, *-] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V, -*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,5) to [R, l^=3.3~k$\Omega$] (5,5);
								\draw (5,5) to [R, l_=5.6~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_{OUT,1}$] (5,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Simplify the circuit by combining the 1.8<nbsp />k&#8486; and 2.4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors in series. This is shown in <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure3" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure3">
  					<caption>The reduced subcircuit of <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure1" /> with the current source deactivated.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure3">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 5V source, 4.2k resistor, 5.1k resistor, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor. The 4.2k resistor, 5.1k resistor, and 3.3k resistors are connected at node b. The 5.1k resistor and high-potential end of the 5V source are connected at node c. The 3.3k resistor and 5.6k resistor are connected at node d. The 1.8k resistor, low-potential end of the 5V source, and 5.6k resistors are connected at node e. Voltage "Vout,1" drops over the 5.6k resistor between nodes d and e.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,5) to [R, l_=4.2~k$\Omega$] (0,0);
								\draw (0,5) to [short] (2.5,5);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] (5,0);
								\draw (2.5,5) to [R, l_=5.1~k$\Omega$, *-] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V, -*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,5) to [R, l^=3.3~k$\Omega$] (5,5);
								\draw (5,5) to [R, l_=5.6~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_{OUT,1}$] (5,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>This is a two-mesh circuit and can be solved in the same manner as <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" />. This may be more obvious if you re-draw the circuit as shown below (the two diagrams are equivalent).</p>
				
				<figure xml:id="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure3b">
  					<caption></caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure3b">
    					<shortdescription>Functionally equivalent circuit diagram to the above.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]					(0,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5.1~k$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3.3~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=5.6~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_{OUT,1}$] (5,0);
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Apply the voltage divider rule as indicated in <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" />.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_{OUT,1} \amp= 5~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{4.2~\textrm{k}\Omega // (3.3~\textrm{k}\Omega + 5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{5.1~\textrm{k}\Omega + (4.2~\textrm{k}\Omega // (3.3~\textrm{k}\Omega + 5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega))}\right)\left(\frac{5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega}{3.3~\textrm{k}\Omega + 5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{4.2~\textrm{k}\Omega // 8.9~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5.1~\textrm{k}\Omega + (4.2~\textrm{k}\Omega // 8.9~\textrm{k}\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega}{8.9~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{2.8534~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5.1~\textrm{k}\Omega + 2.8534~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)(0.6292)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{2.8534~\textrm{k}\Omega}{7.9534~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)(0.6292)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}(0.3588)(0.6292)</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp= 1.13~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Deactivate the voltage source (replace with a short) to determine the contribution to the output voltage due to the current source (<m>V_{OUT,2}</m>). This circuit is shown in <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure5" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure5">
  					<caption>The subcircuit of <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure1" /> with the voltage source deactivated.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure5">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 10mA source, 1.8k resistor, 2.4k resistor, 5.1k resistor, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor. The 1.8k and 2.4k resistors are connected at node a. The 2.4k resistor, 10mA source, 5.1k resistor, and 3.3k resistors are connected at node b. The 10mA source flows out of node b. The 10mA source, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor are connected at node d. The 10mA source flows into node d. The 1.8k resistor, and 5.6k resistors are connected at node e. Voltage "Vout,2" drops over the 5.6k resistor between nodes d and e.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$] (0,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2.4~k$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] (5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5.1~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3.3~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=5.6~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_{OUT,2}$, *-] (5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [I, l^=10~mA] (5,4);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
								\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>The 1.8<nbsp />k&#8486; and 2.4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in series. That combination is in parallel with the 5.1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. The reduced circuit diagram is shown in <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure6" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure6">
  					<caption>The reduced subcircuit of <xref ref="superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure1" /> with the voltage source deactivated.</caption>
  					<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionNumericSubcircuitFigure6">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 10mA source, 2.3k resistor, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor. The 2.3k resistor, 10mA source, and 3.3k resistors are connected at node b. The 10mA source flows out of node b. The 10mA source, 3.3k resistor, and 5.6k resistor are connected at node d. The 10mA source flows into node d. The 2.3k resistor and 5.6k resistors are connected at node e. Voltage "Vout,2" drops over the 5.6k resistor between nodes d and e.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (2.5,0) to [short] (5,0);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.30~k$\Omega$, *-] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3.3~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l_=5.6~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_{OUT,2}$, *-] (5,0);
								\draw (2.5,4) to [I, l^=10~mA] (5,4);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>This example will use a current divider to calculate the current through the 5.6<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. (Feel free to use a different method from <xref ref="ch2-circuit-laws" /> for extra practice.)</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>I_{5.6k} \amp= 10~\textrm{mA} \left(\frac{(5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega+2.30~\textrm{k}\Omega)//3.3~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega+2.30~\textrm{k}\Omega} \right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 10~\textrm{mA} \left(\frac{2.33~\textrm{k}\Omega}{5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega+2.30~\textrm{k}\Omega} \right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 2.95~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_{OUT,2}</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_{OUT,2} \amp= (2.95~\textrm{mA})(5.6~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp= 16.50~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Add the two voltages together to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= V_{OUT,1} + V_{OUT,2}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 1.13~\textrm{V} + 16.50~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 17.6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch3-sourcetransformation">
		<title>Source Transformation</title>

		<p>Source transformation is a process where a current source can be transformed into a voltage source and vice versa. The transformation process changes the source type and the resistor location, but results in an equivalent circuit. The goal of source transformation is usually to reduce a circuit by changing the position of the source and a resistor; this can lead to further reductions using series and parallel combinations of resistors.</p>

		<p>First, it is important to establish what is meant by equivalent circuits. Two equivalent circuits will have identical voltage drops over the load (depicted as <m>V</m> in <xref ref="equivalentCircuits" />) as well as identical currents flowing into the load (depicted as <m>I</m> in <xref ref="equivalentCircuits" />).</p>

		<figure xml:id="equivalentCircuits">
  			<caption>Both of these circuits are equivalent if they have identical voltage drops over the load (<m>V</m>) and current into the load (<m>I</m>).</caption>
  			<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-equivalentCircuits">
    			<shortdescription>Left: A voltage source Vs connected in series with resistor Rs are connected in series with an unknown load circuit. Right: A current source Vs/Rs connected in parallel with resistor Rs are connected in parallel with an unknown load.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]		(0,0);
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$, i^=$I$]	(3,2.5);
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]				(3,0);
						\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v_=$V$]	(2.5,0);
						\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (4.5,1.25) {load\\circuit};
						\draw[]	(8,0) to [I, l^=$V_S/R_S$]				(8,2.5);
						\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [short]					(10,2.5);
						\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [short, i^=$I$]				(11,2.5);
						\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, v^=$V$, *-*]	(10,0);
						\draw[]	(8,0) to [short]						(11,0);
						\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (12.5,1.25) {load\\circuit};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>A voltage source with a value of <m>V_S</m> in series with a resistor with a value of <m>R_S</m> can be transformed into an equivalent circuit with a current source in parallel with that same resistor. Ohm's law is used to determine the value of the current source, which is equal to <m>V_S/R_S</m>. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="voltageTransformToCurrent" />.</p>

		<figure xml:id="voltageTransformToCurrent">
  			<caption>The transformation of a voltage source in series with a resistor (left) to an equivalent circuit consisting of a current source in parallel with a resistor (right).</caption>
  			<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-voltageTransformToCurrent">
    			<shortdescription>Left: A voltage source Vs is connected in series with resistor Rs. The low-potential end of Vs is labeled "b" and the disconnected end of Rs is labeled "a." Right: A current source Vs/Rs is connected in parallel with resistor Rs. Node "a" is connected to the source and resistor, with current flowing into "a." Node "b" is connected to the source and resistor, with current flowing out of "b."</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 		(0,0);
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$, -o]	(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=$V_S/R_S$]		(5,2.5);
						\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]			(7.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]	(7,0);
						\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]			(7.5,0) node[right] {b};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>A current source with a value of <m>I_S</m> in parallel with a resistor with a value of <m>R_S</m> can be transformed into an equivalent circuit with a voltage source in series with that same resistor. Ohm's law is used to determine the value of the voltage source, which is equal to <m>I_SR_S</m>. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="currentTransformToVoltage" />.</p>

		<figure xml:id="currentTransformToVoltage">
  			<caption>The transformation of a current source in parallel with a resistor (left) to an equivalent circuit consisting of a voltage source in series with a resistor (right).</caption>
  			<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-currentTransformToVoltage">
    			<shortdescription>Left: A current source Is is connected in parallel with resistor Rs. Node "a" is connected to the source and resistor, with current flowing into "a." Node "b" is connected to the source and resistor, with current flowing out of "b." Right: A voltage source IsRs is connected in series with resistor Rs. The low-potential end of IsRs is labeled "b" and the disconnected end of Rs is labeled "a." </shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]		(0,2.5);
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]			(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(2,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_S$, *-*]	(2,0);
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$I_SR_S$] 		(5,0);
						\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_S$, -o]	(7.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]			(7.5,0) node[right] {b};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>In either case (converting a voltage source to a current source, or vice versa), the position of the resistor changes but the value of the resistor remains unchanged.</p>

		<p>This process is not limited to independent or DC sources. It is possible to conduct source transformation with any kind of power source used in a linear circuit. That is: source transformation can be achieved with dependent sources or AC sources.</p>

		<example>
			<title>Source transformation example</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Calculate <m>V_X</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="sourceTransformationFigure1" /> using source transformation. The unit of the proportionality constant of the dependent source is A/V.</p>

				<figure xml:id="sourceTransformationFigure1">
  					<caption>Circuit to be solved with source transformation.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-sourceTransformationFigure1">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and three nodes containing a 3mA source, 0.005Vx VCCS, 2k resistor, 3k resistor, and 1k resistor. The 3mA source, 2k resistor, and 3k resistor connect at node A. The 3mA source flows into node A. The 3k resistor, 1k resistor, and 0.005Vx source connect at node B. The 0.005Vx source flows out of node B. The 3mA source, 2k resistor, 1k resistor, and 0.005Vx source connect at node C. The 3mA source flows out of node C. The 0.005Vx source flows into node C. Vx is measured over the 2k resistor with high-potential at node A and low potential at node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=3~mA] (0,2.5);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [cI, l=0.005$V_X$, *-*] (5,0);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [short] (7.5,2.5);
								\draw (7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (7.5,0);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] ++(right:7.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>While it's possible to transform the independent source, it does not yield the most intuitive calculation of <m>V_X</m>. This is because the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor placement would change, which means that <m>V_X</m> would no longer be dropped over the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. To make this more clear, <xref ref="superpositionExampleIndependentSource" /> shows how the placement of <m>V_X</m> would not be changed, while the placement of the resistor does change, if the independent source is transformed to a voltage source.</p>

				<figure xml:id="superpositionExampleIndependentSource">
  					<caption>The placement of <m>V_X</m> does not change if the resistor is moved after transforming the independent source.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-superpositionExampleIndependentSource">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes containing a 6V source, 2k resistor, 3k resistor, 1k resistor, and 0.005Vx VCCS. The high-potential end of the 6V source and 2k connect at node A. The 2k and 3k connect at node B. The 3k, 1k, and 0.005Vx VCCS connect at node C. The VCCS flows out of node C. The low-potential end of the 6V source, 0.005VX VCCS, and 1k resistor connect at node D. The VCCS flows into node C. Vx is measured from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V] (0,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$V_X$] (2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cI, l=0.005$V_X$, *-*] (5,0);
								\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short] (7.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (7.5,0);
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:7.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Instead, the dependent source can be transformed from a VCCS to a VCVS. The new proportionality constant is calculated below.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>k \amp= (0.005~\textrm{A/V})(1000~\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The updated circuit is shown in <xref ref="sourceTransformationFigure2" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="sourceTransformationFigure2">
  					<caption>Circuit after the dependent source is transformed.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-sourceTransformationFigure2">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes containing a 3mA source, 2k resistor, 3k resistor, 1k resistor, and 5Vx VCVS. The 3mA source, 2k resistor, and 3k resistor connect at node A. The 3mA source flows into node A. The 3k resistor and 1k resistor connect at node B. The 1k resistor and low-potential end of the 5Vx source connect at node C. The 3mA source, 2k resistor, and high-potential end of the 5Vx source connect at node D. Vx is measured over the 2k resistor with high-potential at node A and low-potential at node D.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=3~mA] (0,2.5);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw (7.5,0) to [cV, l^=5$V_X$] (7.5,2.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] (7.5,2.5);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] ++(right:7.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>The 3<nbsp />k&#8486; and 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors can be combined in series. This is shown in <xref ref="sourceTransformationFigure3" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="sourceTransformationFigure3">
  					<caption>Reduced circuit after the dependent source is transformed.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-sourceTransformationFigure3">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes containing a 3mA source, 2k resistor, 4k resistor, and 5Vx VCVS. The 3mA source, 2k resistor, and 4k resistor connect at node A. The 3mA source flows into node A. The 4k resistor and low-potential end of the 5Vx VCVS connect at node B. The 3mA source, 2k resistor, and high-potential end of the 5Vx VCVS connect at node C. Vx is measured over the 2k resistor with high-potential at node A and low-potential at node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=3~mA] (0,2.5);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw (5,0) to [cV, l^=5$V_X$] (5,2.5);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] ++(right:5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>At this point, it's possible to use KCL at the node between the independent source, the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, and the 4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor to solve for <m>V_X</m>. (It's also possible to perform another source transformation of the dependent source. This method will be explored after using KCL.)</p>

				<p>The independent source flows into the node. The branch current (defined as flowing in the direction of <m>V_X</m>) through the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor flows out of the node. The branch current through the 4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor will be defined to flow out of the node. This yields the following KCL equation.</p>

				<p>
					<md>3~\textrm{mA} - \frac{V_X - (-5V_X)}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega} - \frac{V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 0</md>
				</p>

				<p>This equation can be solved for <m>V_X</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>0 \amp= 3~\textrm{mA} - \frac{6V_X}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega} - \frac{V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V} - 6V_X - 2V_X</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V} - 8V_X</mrow>
						<mrow>8V_X \amp= 12~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						<mrow>V_X \amp= 1.5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Instead of using KCL, we can instead transform the dependent source from <xref ref="sourceTransformationFigure3" /> back into a VCCS. The new proportionality constant is calculated below.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>g_m \amp= \frac{5}{4000~\Omega}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 0.00125~\textrm{A/V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The re-drawn circuit is shown in <xref ref="sourceTransformationFigure4" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="sourceTransformationFigure4">
  					<caption>Circuit after the dependent source is transformed a second time.</caption>
  					<image width="90%" xml:id="ch3-sourceTransformationFigure4">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with three meshes and two nodes containing a 3mA source, 2k resistor, 4k resistor, and 0.00125Vx VCCS. The 3mA source, 2k, 4k, and VCCS connect at node A. The 3mA source flows into node A. The VCCS flows out of node A. The 3mA source, 2k, 4k, and VCCS connect at node B. The 3mA source flows out of node B. The VCCS flows into node B. Vx is measured over the 2k resistor with high-potential at node A and low-potential at node B.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=3~mA] (0,2.5);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (7.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$, *-*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (7.5,2.5) to [cI, l=0.00125$V_X$] (7.5,0);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$, *-*] (5,0);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] ++(right:7.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>All circuit elements are in parallel. The equivalent resistance consists of the parallel combination of the two resistors. The equivalent source consists of the sum of the two current sources. Note that the unit of the dependent source will have to be converted to mA/V before summing it with the independent source. The equivalent circuit is shown in <xref ref="sourceTransformationFigure5" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="sourceTransformationFigure5">
  					<caption>Equivalent circuit after the dependent source is transformed a second time.</caption>
  					<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-sourceTransformationFigure5">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes containing a (3-1.25Vx) current source and 1.33k resistor. Both elements connect one end at node A and the other at node B. The current source flows into node A and out of node B. Vx is measured over the 1.33k resistor with high-potential at node A and low-potential at node B.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=$(3-1.25~V_X)$~mA] (0,2.5);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1.33~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$] (2.5,0);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_X \amp= [(3-1.25V_X)~\textrm{mA}](1.3~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V} - 1.67V_X</mrow>
						<mrow>2.67V_X \amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						<mrow>V_X \amp= 1.5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

			</statement>
		</example>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch3-choosing-a-tool">
		<title>Choosing an Analysis Tool</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>Students frequently ask how to decide what method to use to solve a circuit analysis problem. Over time, you will build up an intuition on what methods are best for solving different types of questions. The suggestions below are subjective and based on my experience teaching this class. There usually isn't one <q>correct</q> method to solving a problem. Additionally, there are many problems that may benefit from an approach that uses multiple tools instead of just one.</p>

			<p>When a circuit is relatively simple (most or all components are either all in series or all in parallel), tools like Ohm's law, voltage divider, and current divider can be applied to calculate a particular quantity in a circuit. Ohm's law is used when calculating any one particular quantity (voltage, current, resistance) based on the two others. Voltage and current divider can be used based on circuit arrangement. (If you vastly prefer voltage over current dividing, then source transformation can possibly be used to modify the circuit arrangement from parallel to series. This is also true if you vastly prefer current over voltage dividing.)</p>
			
			<p>Once a circuit is more complicated, there are some tools that can be used to solve the circuit without simplification (KCL/KVL and mesh analysis) and some tools that can be used to simplify the circuit (superposition and source transformation), after which point tools such as Ohm's law, voltage divider, and current divider can be applied.</p>
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch3-simplifying-tools">
			<title>Solving via Simplification</title>

			<p>The two major tools to use that involve simplifying a circuit are superposition and source transformation.</p>

			<p><alert>Superposition</alert></p>

			<p>Superposition inherently simplifies any circuit it's applied to, as it eliminates all but one independent source. However, the analysis process must be iterated once per independent source. Superposition is not as helpful when there are dependent sources included in a circuit, as they must be included in all analyses. Superposition is therefore a good tool to use when the circuit has few independent sources.</p>

			<p>Personally, I typically find that the effort that goes into superposition can be better spent either performing source transformation (if the circuit is well-suited to that method) or by solving a circuit wholesale with mesh analysis (particularly if there are dependent sources).</p>

			<p><alert>Source Transformation</alert></p>
			
			<p>Source transformation can be used to greatly simplify any circuit, even those with dependent sources. While there may be many steps involved, they can be applied once (rather than multiple times with superposition) to obtain a reduced circuit.</p>
			
			<p>Source transformation is a good tool to use on circuits where transforming a source will not modify a voltage or current that is either an unknown or the controlling value for a dependent source.</p>
			
			<p>For example, with the switch closed, the circuit shown in <xref ref="ch6-charging-ex5" /> would not be good to use with source transformation, as the controlling current <m>i_X(t)</m> would be modified if the independent source <m>V_X</m> were transformed into a current source.</p>
			
			<p>Source transformation is a good method to use on <xref ref="ch6-general-ex1" />. This is because the sources can be transformed without modifying the unknowns of <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m>. The circuit eventually can be transformed into a circuit with entirely parallel components.</p>
		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch3-calculating-tools">
			<title>Solving without Simplification</title>

			<p>The two major tools that can be used to solve a circuit wholesale without simplifying are KCL/KVL and mesh analysis.</p>

			<p><alert>KCL/KVL</alert></p>
			
			<p>As a method of calculating branch currents in a circuit and obtaining a matrix of unknowns, KCL/KVL is inferior to mesh analysis, as there will always be fewer meshes than branches in a circuit.</p>
			
			<p>There are times when a circuit has a few nodes and loops and may be able to yield a few equations to calculate unknowns using a matrix.</p>
			
			<p><xref ref="ch6-charging-from-zero-example-with-dependent-source" /> is a great example of a circuit that can easily be solved with KCL/KVL. This is because all three branch currents are either defined, unknowns, or can be calculated with Ohm's law from other unknowns, which means KCL is easy to apply at the node connecting each of the three branches. The right-hand loop of the circuit contains an unknown voltage and a voltage source (what I would call a <q>perfect loop</q>), making KVL very easy to apply. KCL/KVL can therefore be applied to this circuit without requiring any simplification, and without having to introduce any new variables.</p>
			
			<p>There are some special cases where KCL and/or KVL should be considered first-case analysis tools.</p>
			
			<p>KCL is nearly always the method used to solve op-amp circuits (<xref ref="ch4-op-amps" />) due to the special property of op-amps to have zero current flowing into their input terminals.</p>
			
			<p>When deriving a differential equation (<xref ref="ch5-capacitors" />, <xref ref="ch6-inductors" />, and <xref ref="ch7-secondorder" />), transfer function (<xref ref="ch9-transferfuncs" />), or doing Laplace analysis (<xref ref="ch11-laplace" />), KCL/KVL is the method that I vastly prefer over all other analysis methods, unless there's a very compelling reason to use another tool (which is only the case where the other tool would allow me to solve the entire circuit in one or two steps).</p>
			
			<p><alert>Mesh Analysis</alert></p>
			
			<p>Mesh analysis as a tool can be used to solve any circuit wholesale, and is typically better than KCL/KVL matrices as there will always be fewer meshes in a circuit than branches. Because mesh analysis can be used to solve any circuit (regardless of complexity), it is great to use to double check a different method.</p>
			
			<p>However, mesh analysis may not be necessary if a circuit isn't terribly complicated. Setting up the mesh analysis equations correctly is vital, as even one mistake setting up the equations will cause the calculations to be incorrect. Therefore, in my opinion, this isn't necessarily a great method to use when simplification procedures can be used instead.</p>
			
			<p><xref ref="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex5" /> is a great example of a circuit solved with mesh analysis. This circuit can't be simplified with superposition (there's only one independent source) and source transformation will modify a controlling voltage, negating the utility of that tool as well. KCL and KVL are unwieldy to apply directly. Therefore, mesh analysis comes out as the best tool.</p>
		</subsection>
	
	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch3-vocisc">
		<title>Open-Circuit Voltages and Short-Circuit Currents</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>Before moving on to more circuit theorems, it is important to discuss the concept of open-circuit voltages and short-circuit currents. These two measurement techniques will be used in abundance when discussing both Th&#233;venin's and Norton's theorems so it is important to have a solid understanding of them.</p>
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch3-voc">
			<title>Open-Circuit Voltage</title>

			<p>Open-circuit voltage (represented as <m>V_{OC}</m> in this textbook) is the potential difference between the two terminals of a circuit that connect to a load <em>when they are disconnected from the load</em>. This is shown for an arbitrary circuit in <xref ref="openCircuitVoltageCircuit" />, if the open-circuit voltage is to be measured between the terminals at nodes a and b.</p>

			<figure xml:id="openCircuitVoltageCircuit">
  				<caption>The open-circuit voltage (<m>V_{OC}</m>) of the circuit on the left can be calculated or measured after it's disconnected from the load, as shown on the right.</caption>
  				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-openCircuitVoltageCircuit">
    				<shortdescription>Left: a circuit containing unknown circuit elements has two terminals labeled a and b that are connected to a load circuit. Right: that same circuit is disconnected from the load; open-circuit voltage Voc drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [esource] (0,2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic] (2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, *-*] (2.5,0);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, -o] (5,2.5) node[above] {a};
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, o-] (6,2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o] (5,0) node[below] {b};
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, o-] (6,0);
							\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=2cm, minimum height=3cm] at (6.5,1.25) {load\\circuit};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(9,0)}]
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [esource] (0,2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic] (2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, *-*] (2.5,0);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, -o] (5,2.5) node[above] {a};
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o] (5,0) node[below] {b};
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$V_{OC}$] (5,0);
							\end{scope}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Once the circuit has been disconnected from the load, use any circuit law or theorem (Ohm's law, voltage or current divider, mesh analysis, KCL/KVL, superposition, source transformation, etc.) to calculate the open-circuit voltage.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating open-circuit voltage</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>V_{OC}</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="openCircuitVoltageFigure1" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="openCircuitVoltageFigure1">
  						<caption>Circuit used to calculate open-circuit voltage.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-openCircuitVoltageFigure1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes containing a 5V source, 3k resistor, 4k resistor, 2k resistor, and 0.8Vx VCVS. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 3k resistor connect at node A. The 3k resistor, 4k resistor, and 2k resistor connect at node B. The 2k resistor and high-potential end of the 0.8Vx VCVS connect at node C. The low-potential end of the 5V source, 4k resistor, and low-potential end of the 0.8Vx VCVS connect at node D. Voltage Vx drops over the 4k resistor from node B to node D. Voltage Voc is measured from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,5) to [V, l_=5~V] (0,0);
									\draw (0,5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$] (2.5,5);
									\draw (2.5,5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$] (2.5,0);
									\draw (2.5,5) to [short, -o] (7.5,5);
									\draw (5,5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-] (5,2.5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [cV, l_=0.8$V_X$, -*] (5,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short, -o] ++(right:7.5);
									\draw (7.5,5) to [open, v^=$V_{OC}$] (7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation will be used to solve this circuit. (Note that KCL/KVL or mesh analysis could also be used to solve this circuit. Try those methods out for extra practice.) Using source transformation, both voltage sources will be transformed into current sources.</p>

					<p>The proportionality constant of the transformed VCCS is calculated below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>g_m \amp= \frac{0.8V_X}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.4~\textrm{mA/V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The transformed circuit is shown in <xref ref="openCircuitVoltageFigure2" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="openCircuitVoltageFigure2">
  						<caption>Equivalent circuit after both sources are transformed.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-openCircuitVoltageFigure2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and two nodes containing a 5/3mA source, 3k resistor, 4k resistor, 2k resistor, and 0.4Vx VCCS. All circuit elements are in parallel with each other. Both current sources flow into node A and out of node B. Voltage Vx drops over the 4k resistor from node A to node B. Voltage Voc is measured from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=5/3~mA] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short, -o] ++(right:12);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=3~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$] (5,0);
									\draw (7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*] (7.5,0);
									\draw (10,0) to [cI, l^=0.4$V_X$, *-*] (10,2.5);
									\draw (0,0) to [short, -o] ++(right:12);
									\draw (12,2.5) to [open, v^=$V_{OC}$] (12,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because all circuit elements are in parallel, a reduced equivalent circuit can be derived. All three resistors can be combined in parallel. The two current sources can be summed to find an equivalent source. The equivalent circuit is shown in <xref ref="openCircuitVoltageFigure3" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="openCircuitVoltageFigure3">
  						<caption>Reduced equivalent circuit after both sources are transformed.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-openCircuitVoltageFigure3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes containing a (3/2+0.4Vx)mA source and a 0.92k resistor. Both circuit elements are in parallel with each other. The source flows into node A and out of node B. Voltage Vx drops over the 0.92k resistor from node A to node B. Voltage Voc is measured from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=$(5/3+0.4V_X)$~mA] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short, -o] ++(right:4.5);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=0.92~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short, -o] ++(right:5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [open, v^=$V_{OC}$] (5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Ohm's law can be used to calculate <m>V_{OC}</m>, which is equal to <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OC} \amp= \left[\left(\frac{5}{3}+0.4V_{OC}\right)~\textrm{mA}\right](0.92~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.54~\textrm{V} + 0.366V_{OC}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.634V_{OC} \amp= 1.54~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_{OC} \amp= 2.4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>
		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch3-isc">
			<title>Short-Circuit Current</title>

			<p>Short-circuit current (represented as <m>I_{SC}</m> in this textbook) is the current between two terminals when they are shorted together. This is shown for a generic circuit in <xref ref="shortCircuitCurrentFigure" />, if the short-circuit current is to be measured between the terminals at nodes a and b.</p>

			<figure xml:id="shortCircuitCurrentFigure">
  				<caption>The short-circuit current (<m>I_{SC}</m>) of the circuit on the left can be calculated or measured after the two terminals are shorted together, as shown on the right.</caption>
  				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-shortCircuitCurrentFigure">
    				<shortdescription>Left: a circuit containing unknown circuit elements has two terminals labeled a and b that are connected to a load circuit. Right: that same circuit is disconnected from the load; short-circuit current Isc flows through a short connecting nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [esource] (-2.5,2.5);
							\draw[] (-2.5,2.5) to [generic] (0,2.5);
							\draw[] (0,2.5) to [generic, *-*] (0,0);
							\draw[] (0,2.5) to [generic, -o] (2.5,2.5) node[above] {a};
							\draw[] (2.5,2.5) to [short, o-] (5,2.5);
							\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [short, -o] (2.5,0) node[below] {b};
							\draw[] (2.5,0) to [short, o-] (5,0);
							\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=2cm, minimum height=3cm] at (4,1.25) {load\\circuit};
							\begin{scope}[shift={(9,0)}]
							\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [esource] (-2.5,2.5);
							\draw[] (-2.5,2.5) to [generic] (0,2.5);
							\draw[] (0,2.5) to [generic, *-*] (0,0);
							\draw[] (0,2.5) to [generic, -o] (2.5,2.5) node[above] {a};
							\draw[] (-2.5,0) to [short, -o] (2.5,0) node[below] {b};
							\draw[] (2.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$, o-o] (2.5,0);
							\end{scope}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Once the circuit has been re-drawn to short out the load, any circuit law or theorem can be used to calculate the short-circuit current.</p>

			<p>At this point, it is important to discuss what happens to shorted-out circuit elements, as this becomes a source of confusion for students.</p>

			<p>If a passive circuit element such as a resistor, capacitor, or inductor is shorted out, the potential difference over that circuit element becomes 0<nbsp />V. Because the voltage drop is zero, Ohm's law dictates that the current will also be zero. If no current flows through a circuit element, and it contains no voltage drop, then that element is essentially absent from the circuit. Therefore, a shorted passive element can be removed from a circuit diagram for the sake of simplicity. This is depicted in <xref ref="passiveShort" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="passiveShort">
  				<caption>Each of these four circuits is functionally equivalent.</caption>
  				<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-passiveShort">
    				<shortdescription>Left: An unknown source is wired in series with an unknown circuit element and a resistor. The resistor is shorted out. The current through the short is labeled Isc. Middle-left: An unknown source is wired in series with an unknown circuit element and a capacitor. The capacitor is shorted out. The current through the short is labeled Isc. Middle-right: An unknown source is wired in series with an unknown circuit element and an inductor. The inductor is shorted out. The current through the short is labeled Isc. Right: An unknown source is wired in series with an unknown circuit element. The current through both circuit elements is labeled Isc.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\foreach \element/\x/\y in {R/0/4, C/5/4, L/0/0} {
								\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [esource] (\x,\y+2.5);
								\draw[]	(\x,\y+2.5) to [generic] ++(right:2.5);
								\draw[]	(\x+2.5,\y+2.5) to [\element, *-*] (\x+2.5,\y);
								\draw[]	(\x+2.5,\y+2.5) to [short] ++(right:1);
								\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [short] ++(right:3.5);
								\draw[]	(\x+3.5,\y+2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$] (\x+3.5,\y);
							}
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [esource]					(5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic]				(7.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$]	(7.5,0);
							\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]					(7.5,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>If a current source is shorted out, the current source continues to contribute current to the circuit. <xref ref="shortedCurrentSource" /> shows an example of a shorted current source. Kirchhoff's current law states that the short-circuit current is equal to the sum of the current coming from the current source added to the current supplied by the branch to the left. The current source is providing a useful function and cannot be removed from the circuit. This is true for all kinds of current sources: AC and DC, independent and dependent.</p>

			<figure xml:id="shortedCurrentSource">
  				<caption>A shorted current source contributes to the short-circuit current and cannot be removed from a circuit.</caption>
  				<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-shortedCurrentSource">
    				<shortdescription>An unknown source is wired in series with an unknown circuit element and an independent current source. The independent current source is shorted out. The current through the short is labeled Isc.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [esource]					(0,2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic]				(2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [I, *-*]					(2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]				(3.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(3.5,0);
							\draw[]	(3.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$]	(3.5,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>If a voltage source is shorted out (depicted in <xref ref="shortedVoltageSource" />), it is also not accurate to say that the voltage source is not contributing anything to the circuit. A shorted voltage source will in fact have a very large impact on a circuit. <alert>Any voltage source that is directly connected to a low- (or no-) resistance conductor will generate massive (or approaching infinite) amounts of current. This would cause the conductor (and possibly the source itself) to heat up, melt, or start on fire.</alert> While it is not a good idea to short a voltage source, it is possible, and it would not be correct to say that the voltage source acts as if it no longer exists.</p>

			<figure xml:id="shortedVoltageSource">
  				<caption>While this would be a very bad idea, the voltage source is still contributing much to the circuit, and cannot be removed.</caption>
  				<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-shortedVoltageSource">
    				<shortdescription>An unknown source is wired in series with an unknown circuit element and an independent voltage source. The independent voltage source is shorted out. The current through the short is labeled Isc.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [esource]					(0,2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic]				(2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, *-*]				(2.5,0);
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(3.5,0);
							\draw[]	(3.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$]	(3.5,0);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]				(3.5,2.5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A voltage source in series with a resistor can be source transformed to a current source in parallel with a resistor. Therefore, if a voltage source in series with a resistor is shorted to calculate a short-circuit current, the source-transformed version is equivalent to a combination of passive element shorted out (the series resistor becomes a parallel resistor, shorted to calculate <m>I_{SC}</m>) and a current source shorted out.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating short-circuit current</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>I_{SC}</m> as depicted in <xref ref="shortCircuitCurrentExample1" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="shortCircuitCurrentExample1">
  						<caption>Circuit used to calculate short-circuit current.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-shortCircuitCurrentExample1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and three nodes containing a 20V source, 5k resistor, 2k resistor, and 5V source. The high-potential end of the 20V source and the 5k resistor connect at node A. The 5k resistor, 2k resistor, and low-potential end of both sources connect at node B. The 2k resistor and high-potential end of the 5V source connect at node C. Current ISC is defined as flowing through node B, from the intersection of the 5k and 2k resistors to the low-potential ends of the two sources.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=20~V]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]					(4.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V, -*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 					++(right:4.5);
									\draw[]	(4.5,5) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$]		(4.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The simplest method to solve this circuit is with KCL at the node where the 5<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, and the short circuit intersect. This is because the voltage at that node must be equal to zero (this is because it is connected to the common potential on both voltage sources). The direction of <m>I_{SC}</m> is defined as flowing out of that node. Because the point of high potential in the other two branches is known, we can see that those two branch currents flow into the node. The calculation is shown below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{5~\textrm{V}}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{mA} + 2.5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>(For extra practice, feel free to try superposition, source transformation, KCL/KVL, or mesh analysis to verify the result.)</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>
	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch3-thevenin">
		<title>Th&#233;venin's Theorem</title>

		<p>Th&#233;venin's theorem provides a very useful way to simplify otherwise complicated circuits. It states that any linear circuit (regardless of the complexity) can be represented by an equivalent circuit that contains a voltage source in series with a resistor. This is depicted in <xref ref="theveninEquivalence" />.</p>

		<figure xml:id="theveninEquivalence">
  			<caption>Th&#233;venin's theorem states that these two circuits are equivalent, given the correct value of <m>V_{TH}</m> and <m>R_{TH}</m>.</caption>
  			<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-theveninEquivalence">
    			<shortdescription>Left: a box representing an unknown linear circuit has two output terminals labeled a and b. Right: a voltage source of value VTH is wired in series with a resistor of value RTH. Terminal a is connected to the resistor. Terminal b is connected to the low-potential end of VTH.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]			(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (0,1.25) {linear\\circuit};
						\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{TH}$]		(5,0);
						\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_{TH}$, -o]	(7.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]			(7.5,0) node[right] {b};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>The technique to Th&#233;venin's theorem is to calculate the correct values of the <term>Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage</term> (<m>V_{TH}</m>) and <term>Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance</term> (<m>R_{TH}</m>). The Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage is equal to the open-circuit voltage between the output terminals (labeled a and b in <xref ref="theveninEquivalence" />). The procedure to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance is given below.</p>

		<p>
			<ul>
				<li><p>If there are no dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, then <m>R_{TH}</m> is equal to the equivalent resistance seen from the output terminals, with all of the independent sources deactivated.</p></li>
				<li><p>If there are dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, calculate the short-circuit current (<m>I_{SC}</m>) between the output terminals. The Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance is equal to <m>V_{TH}/I_{SC}</m>.</p></li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>When working with a dependent source, it's possible that the short-circuit current will be zero, causing the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance to be undefined. In this situation, there is a third method to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance known as the <term>test source method</term>. To use this method, deactivate all independent sources. Place a test source across the two nodes of the circuit. If using a voltage source as a test source, the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance is equal to the test source voltage divided by the current supplied by the source. If using a current source as a test source, the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance is equal to the voltage supplied by the source divided by the test source current.</p>

		<example>
			<title>Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b for the circuit shown in <xref ref="theveninExample1" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="theveninExample1">
  					<caption>Circuit to be converted to a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit.</caption>
  					<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-theveninExample1">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 12V source, 21k resistor, 20k resistor, 28k resistor, 8k resistor, and 15k resistor. The 20k, 8k, and 15k resistors connect at node A. The 15k, 28k, and low-potential end of the 12V source connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 12V source, 20k, and 21k resistors connect at node C. The 21k, 8k, and 28k resistors connect at node D.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,2.5) to [V, l_=12~V] (0,0);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l^=21~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=28~k$\Omega$, -*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l_=15~k$\Omega$, *-*] (5,0);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] ++ (up:1.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [short] ++ (up:1.5);
								\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$] (5,4);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++(right:1) node[right] {a};
								\draw (0,0) to [short, -o] ++(right:6) node[right] {b};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>While it's possible to perform an analysis method such as KCL/KVL or mesh analysis to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage, it's easiest to reduce the circuit using a wye-delta transform. This will make calculating both <m>V_{TH}</m> and <m>R_{TH}</m> easier.</p>

				<p>The equivalent circuit is shown in <xref ref="theveninExample2" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="theveninExample2">
  					<caption>Circuit after a wye-delta transform.</caption>
  					<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-theveninExample2">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 12V source, 20k, 25k, 122.5k, 46.67k, and 15k resistors. The 20k, 35k, 46.67k, and 15k resistors connect at node A. The 15k, 46.67k, 122.5k resistors and the low-potential end of the 12V source connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 12V source, 20k, 35k, and 122.5k resistors connect at node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=12~V]					(0,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=35~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(6,2.5);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=~~122.5~k$\Omega$, -*](3,0);
								\draw[]	(3,0) to [R, l^=46.67~k$\Omega$]		(6,2.5);
								\draw[]	(6,2.5) to [R, l^=15~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(6,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						++ (up:1.5);
								\draw[]	(6,2.5) to [short]						++ (up:1.5);
								\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$]			(6,4);
								\draw[]	(6,2.5) to [short, -o]					++(right:1) node[right] {a};
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]					++(right:7) node[right] {b};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Because the 122.5<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor is in parallel with the voltage source, its contribution to the circuit can be ignored. The 20<nbsp />k&#8486; and 35<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors can be combined in parallel. The 46.67<nbsp />k&#8486; and 15<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors can also be combined in parallel. The reduced circuit is shown in <xref ref="theveninExample3" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="theveninExample3">
  					<caption>Reduced circuit after a wye-delta transform.</caption>
  					<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-theveninExample3">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes containing a 12V source, 12.73k, and 11.35k resistors. The 12.73k and 11.35k resistors connect at node A. The low-potential end of the 12V source and 11.35k resistors connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 12V source and the 12.73k resistor connect at node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,2.5) to [V, l_=12~V] (0,0);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l^=12.73~k$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=11.35~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++(right:1) node[right] {a};
								\draw (0,0) to [short, -o] ++(right:3.5) node[right] {b};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Use a voltage divider to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_{TH} \amp= 12~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{11.35~\textrm{k}\Omega}{12.73~\textrm{k}\Omega+11.35~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 5.66~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>To calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance, deactivate the voltage source and calculate the equivalent resistance seen between terminals a and b. That will be equal to both resistors combined in parallel.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= 12.73~\textrm{k}\Omega//11.35~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit is shown in <xref ref="theveninExample4" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="theveninExample4">
  					<caption>Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit.</caption>
  					<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-theveninExample4">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with zero meshes and three nodes containing a 5.66V source and 6k resistor. The 6k resistor connects at node A. The low-potential end of the 5.66V source connects at node B. The high-potential end of the 5.66V source connects to the 6k resistor at node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,2.5) to [V, l_=5.66~V] (0,0);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$, -o] (2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
								\draw (0,0) to [short, -o] ++(right:2.5) node[right] {b};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

			</statement>
		</example>

		<example>
			<title>Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit using the test source method</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b for the circuit shown in <xref ref="theveninExampleDependent1" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="theveninExampleDependent1">
  					<caption>Circuit with a dependent source to be converted to a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit.</caption>
					<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-theveninExampleDependent1">
						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a CCVS, resistor R, and unknown circuit element. The magnitude of the CCVS is rm*Ix. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R at node C. R connects to one end of the unknown circuit element at node A (node a of the Thevenin circuit). The other end of the unknown circuit element connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node B (node b of the Thevenin circuit.) Branch current Ix flows through the unknown circuit element from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\def\H{2.5}
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [cV, l^=$r_mI_X$] ++(up:\H) to [R, l^=$R$, -o] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a} to [short, o-] ++(right:1) to [generic, i_=$I_X$] ++(down:\H) to [short, -o] ++ (left:1) node[below] {b} to [short, o-] ++(left:\H);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>
		
				<p>If nodes a and b are shorted, <m>I_X</m> becomes equal to zero, the dependent source provides no voltage, and the short-circuit current is equal to zero. This would cause the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance to be undefined. A test voltage source will be placed between nodes a and b.</p>

				<figure>
					<caption></caption>
					<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-theveninExampleDependent2">
						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes consisting of a CCVS, resistor R, and test voltage source Vtest. The magnitude of the CCVS is rm*Ix. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R at node C. R connects to the high-potential end of Vtest at node A (node a of the Thevenin circuit). The low-potential end of Vtest connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node B (node b of the Thevenin circuit.) Branch current Ix flows through Vtest from node A to node B. Branch current Itest flows through R from node A to node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\def\H{2.5}
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [cV, l^=$r_mI_X$] ++(up:\H) to [R, l^=$R$, -o] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a};
								\draw[]	(\H+1,\H) to [short, -o, i_=$I_{test}$] ++(left:1);
								\draw[]	(\H+1,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{test}$] ++(down:\H) to [short, -o] ++ (left:1) node[below] {b} to [short, o-] ++(left:\H);
								\draw[]	(\H+1,\H) to [open, i_=$I_X$] ++(down:\H);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Use KVL to calculate the voltage drops on the left side of the circuit and equate them to <m>V_{test}</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>V_{test} = -r_mI_X - RI_X</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use KCL to calculate the test source current.</p>

				<p>
					<md>I_{test} = -I_X</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{test}}{I_{test}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{-r_mI_X - RI_X}{-I_X}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= r_m + R</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>There are no independent sources. Therefore, the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage is zero. The Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit is shown below.</p>

				<figure>
					<caption></caption>
					<image width="30%" xml:id="ch3-theveninExampleDependent3">
						<shortdescription>Circuit with zero meshes and two nodes consisting of one resistor of value rm+R. One end of the resistor is node A. The other end of the resistor is node B.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\def\H{2.5}
								\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short] ++(down:\H) to [short, -o] ++(right:\H) node[below] {b};
								\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, -o, l^=$r_m + R$] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>
		
			</statement>
		</example>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch3-norton">
		<title>Norton's Theorem</title>

		<p>Norton's theorem states that any linear circuit, regardless of the level of complexity, can be represented by an equivalent circuit that contains a current source in parallel with a resistor. This is depicted in <xref ref="nortonEquivalentCircuit" />.</p>

		<figure xml:id="nortonEquivalentCircuit">
  			<caption>Norton's theorem states that these two circuits are equivalent, given the correct value of <m>I_N</m> and <m>R_N</m>.</caption>
  			<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-nortonEquivalentCircuit">
    			<shortdescription>Left: a box representing an unknown linear circuit has two output terminals labeled a and b. Right: a current source of value In is wired in parallel with a resistor of value Rn. Terminal a is connected to the resistor and source (the source flows into terminal a). Terminal b is connected to the resistor and the source (the source flows out of terminal b).</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]			(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(2.5,0) node[right] {b};
						\node[fill=white, draw, align=center, minimum width=3cm, minimum height=3cm] at (0,1.25) {linear\\circuit};
						\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=$I_N$]			(5,2.5);
						\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_N$, *-*]	(7.5,0);
						\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]			(8.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
						\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o]			(8.5,0) node[right] {b};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>The technique to Norton's theorem is in calculating the <term>Norton equivalent current</term> (<m>I_N</m>) and <term>Norton equivalent resistance</term> (<m>R_N</m>). The Norton equivalent current is equal to the short-circuit current between the output terminals (labeled a and b in <xref ref="nortonEquivalentCircuit" />). The procedure to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance is given below.</p>

		<p>
			<ul>
				<li><p>If there are no dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, then <m>R_{N}</m> is equal to the equivalent resistance seen between the output terminals, with all of the independent sources deactivated.</p></li>
				<li><p>If there are dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, calculate the open-circuit voltage (<m>V_{OC}</m>) between the output terminals. The Norton equivalent resistance is equal to <m>V_{OC}/I_N</m>.</p></li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>The test source method can also be used in solving Norton equivalent circuits for the same reasons that it would be used in solving Th&#233;venin equivalent circuits.</p>

		<p>It can be noted that Norton's theorem and Th&#233;venin's theorem are functionally equivalent to each other; one is simply the source-transformed version of the other. It is therefore possible to find a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit and do a source transformation to derive the Norton equivalent circuit.</p>

		<example>
			<title>Norton equivalent circuit</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Derive the Norton equivalent circuit between nodes a and b for the circuit shown in <xref ref="nortonExampleFigure1" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="nortonExampleFigure1">
  					<caption>Circuit to be converted to a Norton equivalent circuit.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-nortonExampleFigure1">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 16V source, 4Ix CCCS, 4ohm, 12ohm, 5ohm, and 2ohm resistor. The 4ohm and 12ohm resistors connect at node A. The 5ohm resistor connects at node B. The high-potential end of the 16V source connects to the 4ohm resistor at node C. The 5ohm, 2ohm, and 4Ix CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. The low-potential end of the 16V source, 12ohm, 2ohm, and CCCS connect at node E. The CCCS flows out of node E. Ix is defined through the 12ohm resistor and flows out of node A and into node E.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=16~V] (0,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$, *-*] (2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++(right:0.5) node[above] {A};
								\draw[]	(4,2.5) node[above] {B} to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$, o-] (6.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, *-*] (6.5,0);
								\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [short] (9,2.5);
								\draw[]	(9,0) to [cI, l^=4$I_X$] (9,2.5);
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] (9,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Because there is a dependent source, <m>R_N</m> cannot be calculated directly. We will need to calculate <m>V_{OC}</m> and <m>I_N</m>. The open-circuit voltage is equal to</p>

				<p>
					<md>V_{OC} = V_A - V_B.</md>
				</p>

				<p>To calculate <m>V_A</m>, note that the left side of the circuit that defines <m>V_A</m> is disconnected from the rest of the circuit. This quantity can be calculated using the voltage divider rule.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_A \amp= 16~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{12~\Omega}{12~\Omega+4~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>To calculate <m>V_B</m>, <m>I_X</m> needs to be known. Ohm's law can be performed on the left side of the circuit to calculate this.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>I_X \amp= \frac{16~\textrm{V}}{4~\Omega + 12~\Omega}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 1~\textrm{A}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p><m>V_B</m> is equal to the voltage dropped over the 2<nbsp />&#8486;resistor. Use Ohm's law to calculate this value.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_B \amp= (4*I_X)(2~\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= (4~\textrm{A})(2~\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
					
				</p>

				<p><m>V_{OC}</m> can now be calculated.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_{OC} \amp= V_A - V_B</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V} - 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Now we must short terminals a and b to calculate the Norton equivalent current. The circuit diagram is shown in <xref ref="nortonExampleFigure2" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="nortonExampleFigure2">
  					<caption>Circuit with terminals a and b shorted to calculate <m>I_N</m>.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-nortonExampleFigure2">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 16V source, 4Ix CCCS, 4ohm, 12ohm, 5ohm, and 2ohm resistor. The 4ohm, 12ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node A. The high-potential end of the 16V source connects to the 4ohm resistor at node C. The 5ohm, 2ohm, and 4Ix CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. The low-potential end of the 16V source, 12ohm, 2ohm, and CCCS connect at node E. The CCCS flows out of node E. Ix is defined through the 12ohm resistor and flows out of node A and into node E. In is defined through the 5ohm resistor and flows out of node A and into node D.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=16~V] (0,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$, *-*] (2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$, i^=$I_N$] (6.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, *-*] (6.5,0);
								\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [short] (9,2.5);
								\draw[]	(9,0) to [cI, l^=4$I_X$] (9,2.5);
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] (9,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Mesh analysis will be the simplest method to solve this circuit. (There are fewer meshes than branches, making mesh analysis simpler than KCL/KVL. Source transformation, while it could be used to reduce the circuit somewhat, cannot be used to reduce the circuit to one where a simple voltage divider or Ohm's law can be used. This is because branch currents are defined through resistors that would be modified in the source transformation process.)</p>

				<p>KVL will be performed on each of the three meshes (clockwise mesh currents are numbered 1<mdash />3 from left to right), and the dependent source relationship will be used as a fourth equations. These equations are listed below.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>-16 + 4I_1 + 12(I_1 - I_2) \amp= 0</mrow>
						<mrow>12(I_2 - I_1) + 5I_2 + 2(I_2-I_3) \amp= 0</mrow>
						<mrow>-4I_X \amp= I_3</mrow>
						<mrow>I_1 - I_2 \amp= I_X</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_1</m>, then <m>I_2</m>, <m>I_3</m>, and <m>I_X</m>. The right column contains constants.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						\begin{bmatrix} 
							16	\amp -12	\amp 0	\amp 0	\amp 16\\
							-12	\amp 19		\amp -2	\amp 0	\amp 0\\
							0	\amp 0		\amp 1	\amp 4	\amp 0\\
							1	\amp -1		\amp 0	\amp -1	\amp 0\\
						\end{bmatrix}
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						\begin{bmatrix} 
							1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0	\amp 0	\amp 1.375\\
							0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0	\amp 0	\amp 0.5\\
							0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 0	\amp -3.5\\
							0	\amp 0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 0.875\\
						\end{bmatrix}
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The Norton equivalent current is equal to mesh current <m>I_2</m>. Therefore, <m>I_N</m> is 0.5<nbsp />A.</p>

				<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>R_N \amp= \frac{4~\textrm{V}}{0.5~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 8~\Omega</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>The Norton equivalent circuit is shown in <xref ref="nortonExampleFigure3" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="nortonExampleFigure3">
  					<caption>Norton equivalent circuit.</caption>
  					<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-nortonExampleFigure3">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and two nodes containing a 0.5A source and 8ohm resistor. The source and resistor connect at node A. The source flows into node A. The source and resistor connect at node B. The source flows into node B.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=0.5~A]					(0,2.5);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]					(3.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]					(3.5,0) node[right] {b};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

			</statement>
		</example>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch3-maxpower">
		<title>Maximum Power Transfer</title>

		<p>The theorem of maximum power transfer states that the maximum amount of power will be delivered to the load when the resistance of the load is equal to the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance of the circuit. The circuit shown in <xref ref="maximumPowerFigure" /> will be used to demonstrate this theorem.</p>

		<figure xml:id="maximumPowerFigure">
  			<caption>Circuit diagram used to demonstrate the theorem of maximum power transfer.</caption>
  			<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-maximumPowerFigure">
    			<shortdescription>Circuit containing a voltage source of value Vth wired in series with a resistor of value Rth and another resistor of value Rload. Current I flows through the circuit from high-potential to low-potential.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{TH}$]				(0,0);
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_{TH}$, -o, i^=$I$]	(2.5,2.5);
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]					(2.5,0);
						\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_{LOAD}$, o-o]		(2.5,0);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>To determine the value of the resistance of the load (<m>R_{LOAD}</m>) that will lead to maximum power transfer, the power consumed by the load (<m>P_{LOAD}</m>) can be calculated using <xref ref="ohmspower1" />. This is shown in <xref ref="maxPowerEquation" /> and derived in <xref ref="maxPowerEquationDerivation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="maxPowerEquation">P_{LOAD} = \frac{R_{LOAD}V_{TH}^2}{(R_{TH}+R_{LOAD})^2}</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="maxPowerEquationDerivation">
			<p>Ohm's law is used to calculate <m>I_{LOAD}</m> and the voltage divider rule is used to calculate <m>V_{LOAD}</m>.</p>

			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= \frac{V_{TH}}{R_{TH}+R_{LOAD}}</mrow>
					<mrow>V_{LOAD} \amp= V_{TH} \left( \frac{R_{LOAD}}{R_{TH}+R_{LOAD}} \right)</mrow>
					<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= I_{LOAD}V_{LOAD}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_{LOAD}V_{TH}^2}{(R_{TH}+R_{LOAD})^2}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>

		<p>The derivative of <xref ref="maxPowerEquation" /> can be taken with respect to <m>R_{LOAD}</m>. This equation can be set equal to zero to calculate the maximum of the equation. (The full derivation will not be shown in this book.) <alert>Maximum power transfer occurs when <m>R_{LOAD}</m> is equal to <m>R_{TH}</m>.</alert></p>

		<p>The maximum power that can be consumed by the load can then be calculated. This value is defined in <xref ref="theMaximumPower" /> and derived in <xref ref="theMaximumPowerDerivation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="theMaximumPower">P_{MAX} = \frac{V_{TH}^2}{4R_{TH}}</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="theMaximumPowerDerivation">
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= \frac{R_{TH}V_{TH}^2}{(R_{TH}+R_{TH})^2}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_{TH}V_{TH}^2}{(2R_{TH})^2}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_{TH}V_{TH}^2}{4R_{TH}^2}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{V_{TH}^2}{4R_{TH}}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>


		<p>It is important to note that while the maximum <em>amount</em> of power is transferred when the load resistance is equal to the Th&#233;venin resistance, this does not equate to the maximum load power <em>efficiency</em> (ratio of power delivered to the load to the total power). In the case of maximum power transfer, the power efficiency is limited to 50%.</p>

		<example>
			<title>Maximum power transfer</title>

			<statement>
				<p>Calculate the value of the load resistor (<m>R_{LOAD}</m>) that is required for maximum power transfer given the circuit shown in <xref ref="maxPowerFigure1" />. Then, calculate the maximum power transferred to that load.</p>

				<figure xml:id="maxPowerFigure1">
  					<caption>Circuit for calculating maximum power transfer.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-maxPowerFigure1">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 24V source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, 8k resistor, 2k resistor, and Rload resistor. The high-potential end of the 24V source connects to the 4k resistor at node A. The 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, and 8k resistor connect at node B. The CCCS flows into node B. The 8k, 2k, and Rload resistors connect at node C. The low-potential end of the 24V source, 2Ix CCCS, 2k, and Rload resistors connect at node D. The CCCS flows out of node D. Current Ix is defined through the 2k resistor and flows out of node C and into node D.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V] (0,0);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,0) to [cI, l^=2$I_X$, *-*] (2.5,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*, i=$I_X$] (5,0);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [short, -o] (6,2.5);
								\draw (0,0) to [short, -o] (6,0);
								\draw (6,0) to [short, o-] (7,0);
								\draw (6,2.5) to [short, o-] (7,2.5);
								\draw (7,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_{LOAD}$] (7,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Convert the circuit to a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit. Use source transformation on the 24<nbsp />V voltage source to convert it to a 6<nbsp />mA current source. The source transformed circuit is shown in <xref ref="maxPowerFigure2" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="maxPowerFigure2">
  					<caption>Source transformed circuit.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-maxPowerFigure2">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 6mA source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, 8k resistor, and 2k resistor. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, and 8k resistor connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. The 8k and 2k resistors connect at node B. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, 2Ix CCCS, and 2k resistor connect at node C. Both current sources flow out of node C. </shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA] (0,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (5,0) to [cI, l^=2$I_X$, *-*] (5,2.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~k$\Omega$] (7.5,2.5);
								\draw (7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$, v^=$V_{TH}$] (7.5,0);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] ++(right:7.5);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] ++(right:5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Because the two current sources are in parallel, they can be combined into an equivalent source. The updated circuit is shown in <xref ref="maxPowerFigure3" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="maxPowerFigure3">
  					<caption>Reduced source transformed circuit.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-maxPowerFigure3">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a (6mA + 2Ix) current source, 4k resistor, 8k resistor, and 2k resistor. The current source, 4k resistor, and 8k resistor connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 8k and 2k resistors connect at node B. The current source, 4k, and 2k resistors connect at node C. The current source flows out of node C. Ix is defined through Ix and flows from node B to node C. Vth drops over the 2k resistor and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA+2$I_X$]							(0,2.5);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~k$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$, v^=$V_{TH}$]	(5,0);
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>I_X</m>. First, calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (4~\textrm{k}\Omega)//(2~\textrm{k}\Omega + 8~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= (4~\textrm{k}\Omega)//(10~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 2.857~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Now <m>I_X</m> can be calculated.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>I_X \amp= \left(6~\textrm{mA} +2I_X\right)\left(\frac{2.857~\textrm{k}\Omega}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 1.714~\textrm{mA} + 0.571I_X</mrow>
						<mrow>0.429I_X \amp= 1.714~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						<mrow>I_X \amp= 4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_{TH}</m>.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_{TH} \amp= (4~\textrm{mA})(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Calculate the short circuit current. Because the resistor with the controlling current is shorted, the controlling current is 0<nbsp />mA (no current will flow through the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor when it can travel through a zero-resistance short instead), and the dependent source is effectively deactivated. The circuit diagram depicting this is shown in <xref ref="maxPowerFigure4" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="maxPowerFigure4">
  					<caption>Circuit with load short-circuited.</caption>
  					<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-maxPowerFigure4">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes containing a 6mA source, 4k resistor, 0mA CCCS, and 8k resistor. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, CCCS, and 8k resistor connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. All circuit elements also connect at node B. Both current sources flow out of node B. Current Isc is defined through the 8k resistor and flows from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA] (0,2.5);
								\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
								\draw (5,0) to [cI, l^=0, *-*] (5,2.5);
								\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~k$\Omega$] (7.5,2.5);
								\draw (7.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$] (7.5,0);
								\draw (0,0) to [short] ++(right:7.5);
								\draw (0,2.5) to [short] ++(right:5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>This circuit reduces to the one shown in <xref ref="maxPowerFigure5" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="maxPowerFigure5">
  					<caption>Reduced circuit with load short-circuited.</caption>
  					<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-maxPowerFigure5">
    					<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes containing a 6mA source, 4k resistor, and 8k resistor. The 6mA source, 4k resistor, and 8k resistor connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. All circuit elements also connect at node B. The current source flows out of node B. Current Isc is defined through the 8k resistor and flows from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]					(0,2.5);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
								\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=8~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
								\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$]			(5,0);
								\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:5);
								\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>Use a current divider to calculate the short-circuit current.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= 6~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{8~\textrm{k}\Omega//4~\textrm{k}\Omega}{8~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{2~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Therefore, the resistor for maximum power transfer is 4<nbsp />k&#8486;. Use <xref ref="theMaximumPower" /> to calculate the maximum power transferred to this load.</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= \frac{(8~\textrm{V})^2}{4(4~\textrm{k}\Omega)}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{mW}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

			</statement>
		</example>

		<p>Given a Norton equivalent circuit, the maximum amount of power will be delivered to the load when the resistance of the load is equal to the Norton equivalent resistance of the circuit. The circuit shown in <xref ref="maximumPowerFigureNorton" /> will be used to demonstrate this theorem.</p>

		<figure xml:id="maximumPowerFigureNorton">
  			<caption>Circuit diagram used to demonstrate the theorem of maximum power transfer with a Norton equivalent circuit.</caption>
  			<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-maximumPowerFigureNorton">
    			<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes containing a In current source, Rn resistor, and Rload resistor. All circuit elements are wired in parallel. All circuit elements connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. All circuit elements connect at node B. The current source flows out of node B. Current I is defined through the Rload resistor and flows from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_N$]						(0,2.5);
						\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_N$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
						\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_{LOAD}$, i^=$I$, o-o]	(5,0);
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]						(5,0);
						\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]						(5,2.5);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>To determine the value of the resistance of the load (<m>R_{LOAD}</m>) that will lead to maximum power transfer, the power consumed by the load (<m>P_{LOAD}</m>) can be calculated using <xref ref="ohmspower1" />. This is shown in <xref ref="maxPowerEquationNorton" /> and derived in <xref ref="maxPowerEquationDerivationNorton" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="maxPowerEquationNorton">P_{LOAD} = \frac{R_{LOAD}R_N^2I_N^2}{(R_N+R_{LOAD})^2}</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="maxPowerEquationDerivationNorton">
			<p>Ohm's law is used to calculate <m>V_{LOAD}</m> and the current divider rule is used to calculate <m>I_{LOAD}</m>.</p>

			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>I_{LOAD} \amp= \frac{I_NR_N}{R_N+R_{LOAD}}</mrow>
					<mrow>V_{LOAD} \amp= I_N \left( \frac{R_{LOAD}R_N}{R_N+R_{LOAD}} \right)</mrow>
					<mrow>P_{LOAD} \amp= I_{LOAD}V_{LOAD}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{I_N^2R_N^2R_{LOAD}}{(R_N+R_{LOAD})^2}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>

		<p>The derivative of <xref ref="maxPowerEquationNorton" /> can be taken with respect to <m>R_{LOAD}</m>. This equation can be set equal to zero to calculate the maximum of the equation. (The full derivation will not be shown in this book.) <alert>Maximum power transfer occurs when <m>R_{LOAD}</m> is equal to <m>R_N</m>.</alert></p>

		<p>The maximum power that can be consumed by the load can then be calculated. This value is defined in <xref ref="theMaximumPowerNorton" /> and derived in <xref ref="theMaximumPowerDerivationNorton" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="theMaximumPowerNorton">P_{MAX} = \frac{I_N^2R_N}{4}</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="theMaximumPowerDerivationNorton">
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= \frac{I_N^2R_N^2R_{LOAD}}{(R_N+R_{LOAD})^2}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{I_N^2R_N^2R_N}{(2R_N)^2}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{I_N^2R_N^3}{4R_N^2}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{I_N^2R_N}{4}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>

	</section>

	<exercises>
    	<title>Practice Problems</title>

    	<subexercises>
			<title>Superposition</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch3-superpos-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit31fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit31fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3-superpos-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-circuit31fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 13V source, 390ohm resistor, 560ohm resistor, and 22mA source. The high-potential end of the 13V source and 390ohm resistor connect at node A. The 390ohm, 560ohm, and 22mA source connect at node B. The 22mA source flows into node B. The low-potential end of the 13V source, 560ohm, and 22mA source connect at node C. The 22mA source flows out of node C. Voltage Vx is defined over the 560ohm resistor and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=13~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=390~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=560~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l_=22~mA]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>12.7<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit with only the voltage source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the voltage source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit31figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes containing a 13V source, 390ohm resistor, and 560ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 13V source and 390ohm resistor connect at node A. The 390ohm and 560ohm resistors connect at node B. The low-potential end of the 13V source and 560ohm resistors connect at node C. Voltage Vx1 is defined over the 560ohm resistor and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=13~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=390~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=560~$\Omega$, v^=$V_{X1}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_{X1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X1} \amp= 13~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{560~\Omega}{390~\Omega+560~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.66~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit with only the current source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-circuit31figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes containing a 390ohm resistor, 560ohm resistor, and 22mA source. The 390ohm, 560ohm, and 22mA source connect at node A. The 22mA source flows into node A. The 390ohm, 560ohm, and 22mA source connect at node B. The 22mA source flows out of node B. Voltage Vx2 is defined over the 560ohm resistor and drops from node B to node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=390~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=560~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_{X2}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l_=22~mA]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the resistors in parallel and use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_{X2}</m>. (Because the two resistors are in parallel, <m>V_{X2}</m> drops over both resistors.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X2} \amp= (560~\Omega//390~\Omega)(0.022~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (229.89~\Omega)(0.022~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.06~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Add the two values to calculate the voltage <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= V_{X1} + V_{X2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.66~\textrm{V} + 5.06~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12.7~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3-superpos-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit32fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit32fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3-superpos-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit32fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes containing a 20V source, 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 24mA source, 200ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source and 100ohm resistor connect at node A. The 100ohm, 400ohm, and 24mA source connect at node B. The 24mA source flows into node B. The 400ohm, 24mA source, and 200ohm connect at node C. The 24mA source flows out of node C. The 200ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node D. The 600ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node E. The 700ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node F. Vx is defined over the 700ohm resistor and drops from node F to node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [I, l_=24~mA, *-*]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]				(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_X$]		(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p><minus />3.64<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit with only the voltage source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the voltage source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit32figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 6 nodes containing a 20V source, 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source and 100ohm resistor connect at node A. The 100ohm and 400ohm resistors connect at node B. The 400ohm and 200ohm resistors connect at node C. The 200ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node D. The 600ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node E. The 700ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node F. Vx1 is defined over the 700ohm resistor and drops from node F to node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X1}$]		(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine all resistors <em>except for the one that drops <m>V_X</m></em> in series and re-draw (this step can be skipped if you note that all resistors are in series and the voltage divider rule can be applied without combining any resistors).</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit with only the voltage source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit32figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes containing a 20V source, 1300ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source and 1300ohm resistor connect at node A. The 1300ohm resistor and 700ohm resistor connect at node B. The 700ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node C. Voltage Vx1 is defined over the 700ohm resistor and drops from node C to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1300~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X1}$]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_{X1}</m>. Note that the sign is negative because the voltage drop <m>V_{X1}</m> opposes the direction of the voltage source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X1} \amp= -20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{700~\Omega}{1300~\Omega+700~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -7~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit with only the current source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit32figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 24mA source, 200ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. The 100ohm resistor and 700ohm resistor connect at node A. The 100ohm, 400ohm, and 24mA source connect at node B. The 24mA source flows into node B. The 400ohm, 24mA source, and 200ohm connect at node C. The 24mA source flows out of node C. The 200ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node D. The 600ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node E. Voltage Vx is defined over the 700ohm resistor and drops from node A to node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [I, l_=24~mA, *-*]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]				(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X2}$]	(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine resistors in series <em>except for the one that drops <m>V_X</m></em> and re-draw. (This means the 100<nbsp />&#8486;, 200<nbsp />&#8486;, and 600<nbsp />&#8486; resistors will be combined into a single equivalent resistance of 900<nbsp />&#8486;.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit with only the current source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch3-circuit32figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 900ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 24mA source, and 700ohm resistor. The 400ohm resistor, 700ohm resistor, and 24mA source connect at node A. The 24mA source flows into node A. The 400ohm, 24mA source, and 900ohm connect at node B. The 24mA source flows out of node B. The 900ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node C. Voltage Vx is defined over the 700ohm resistor and drops from node A to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [I, l_=24~mA, *-*]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]					(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(0,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=900~$\Omega$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X2}$]		(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate the current through the branch containing the 700<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and 900<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I \amp= 24~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{400~\Omega//(700~\Omega+900~\Omega)}{700~\Omega+900~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{400~\Omega//1600~\Omega}{1600~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{320~\Omega}{1600~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.8~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_{X2}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X2} \amp= (4.8~\textrm{mA})(0.7~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.36~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Add the two values to calculate the voltage <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= V_{X1} + V_{X2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -7~\textrm{V} + 3.36~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -3.64~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3-superpos-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit33fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit33fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3-superpos-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit33fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 24V source, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node A. The 6mA source flows into node A. The 12k resistor and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node C. The 1k resistor and 10k resistor connect at node D. The 10k resistor, low-potential end of the 24V source, 18k resistor, and 6mA source connect at node E. The 6mA source flows out of node E. Current Ix is defined through the 10k resistor and flows out of node D and into node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]							(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]								(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=18~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=12~k$\Omega$, *-]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(10,5);
									\draw[]	(10,5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>3.44<nbsp />mA</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit with only the current source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit33figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, and 1k resistor connect at node A. The 6mA source flows into node A. The 1k resistor and 10k resistor connect at node B. The 10k resistor, 12k resistor, 18k resistor, and 6mA source connect at node C. The 6mA source flows out of node C. Current Ix1 is defined through the 10k resistor and flows out of node B and into node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=18~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~k$\Omega$, *-]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_{X1}$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>I_{X1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{X1} \amp= 6~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{18~\textrm{k}\Omega//12~\textrm{k}\Omega//(10~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{18~\textrm{k}\Omega//12~\textrm{k}\Omega//11~\textrm{k}\Omega}{11~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{4.35~\textrm{k}\Omega}{11~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.3736~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit with only the 24<nbsp />V source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the 24<nbsp />V source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-circuit33figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing an 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 24V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 18k resistor, 12k resistor, and l1k resistor connect at node A. The 12k resistor and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node B. The 1k resistor and 10k resistor connect at node C. The 10k resistor, low-potential end of the 24V source, and 18k resistor connect at node D. Current Ix2 is defined through the 10k resistor and flows out of node C and into node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=18~k$\Omega$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]								(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=12~k$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_{X2}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule for a two-mesh circuit, as demonstrated in <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" />, to calculate the voltage dropped over the 10<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. (It's also possible to perform a source transformation and then use the current divider rule to calculate <m>I_{X2}</m>.) The circuit is re-drawn below to make it more clear how the circuit corresponds to the one shown in <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" />.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Rearranged circuit with only the 24<nbsp />V source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-circuit33figans2re">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing an 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 24V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 12k resistor and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node A. The 18k resistor, 12k resistor, and 1k resistor connect at node B. The 1k resistor and 10k resistor connect at node C. The 10k resistor, low-potential end of the 24V source, and 18k resistor connect at node D. Current Ix2 is defined through the 10k resistor and flows out of node C and into node D. Voltage V18 is defined over the 18k resistor and drops from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V]											(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=12~k$\Omega$]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=18~k$\Omega$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i_=$I_{X2}$, v^=$V_{10k}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{10k} \amp= 24~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{18~\textrm{k}\Omega // (1~\textrm{k}\Omega + 10~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{12~\textrm{k}\Omega + (18~\textrm{k}\Omega // (1~\textrm{k}\Omega + 10~\textrm{k}\Omega))}\right)\left(\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega + 10~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{18~\textrm{k}\Omega // 11~\textrm{k}\Omega}{12~\textrm{k}\Omega + (18~\textrm{k}\Omega // 11~\textrm{k}\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}{11~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{6.8276~\textrm{k}\Omega}{12~\textrm{k}\Omega + 6.8276~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)(0.9091)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{6.8276~\textrm{k}\Omega}{18.8276~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)(0.9091)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 24~\textrm{V}(0.3626)(0.9091)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.9121~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_{X2}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{X2} \amp= \frac{7.9121~\textrm{V}}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.7912~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit with only the 5<nbsp />V source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the 5<nbsp />V source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit33figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing an 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 18k resistor, 12k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node A. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node B. The 1k resistor and 10k resistor connect at node C. The 10k resistor, 12k resistor, and 18k resistor connect at node D. Current Ix3 is defined through the 10k resistor and flows out of node C and into node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=18~k$\Omega$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_{X3}$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor is in parallel with the 18<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, so they can be combined. That equivalent resistance of 7.2<nbsp />k&#8486; can be combined in series with the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor and the 10<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. Re-draw the reduced circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit with only the 5<nbsp />V source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch3-circuit33figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes containing an 18.2k resistor and 5V source. The 18.2k resistor and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node A. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 18.2k resistor connect at node B. Current Ix3 is defined through the 18.2k resistor and flows out of node B and into node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=18.2~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_{X3}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_{X3}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{X3} \amp= \frac{5~\textrm{V}}{18.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.2747~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Add the three values to calculate the current <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= I_{X1} + I_{X2} + I_{X3}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.3736~\textrm{mA} + 0.7912~\textrm{mA} + 0.2747~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.44~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3-superpos-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit34fig" />. The unit of the proportionality constant on the VCCS is A/V.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit34fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3-superpos-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 40V source, 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The high-potential end of the 40V source and 10ohm resistor (10A) connect at node A. The 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node B. The VCCS flows into node B. The 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. The 2A source flows into node C. The low-potential end of the 40V source, 0.3Vx VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node D. Both current sources flow out of node D. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=40~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.3$V_X$, *-*]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=2~A]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>50<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit with only the voltage source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the voltage source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 40V source, 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx1 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10C), and 10ohm resistor (10D). The high-potential end of the 40V source and 10ohm resistor (10A) connect at node A. The 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx1 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node B. The VCCS flows into node B. The 10ohm resistor (10C) and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node C. The low-potential end of the 40V source, 0.3Vx1 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node D. The VCCS flows out of node D. Voltage Vx1 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=40~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.3$V_{X1}$, *-*]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, v^=$V_{X1}$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because there are no voltages over or branch currents through any of the resistors close to the sources, source transformation will be a beneficial way to solve this circuit. (It's also possible to use KCL/KVL or mesh analysis. Try those methods for additional practice.)</p>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a current source and re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the voltage source activated after a source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 4A source, 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx1 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10C), and 10ohm resistor (10D). The 4A source, 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx1 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node A. The 4A source and VCCS flow into node A. The 10ohm resistor (10C) and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node B. The 4A source, 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx1 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node C. The 4A source and VCCS flow out of node C. Voltage Vx1 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=4~A]							(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.3$V_{X1}$, *-*]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, v^=$V_{X1}$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources are in parallel and can be combined. Two of the 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined. The circuit is re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit with only the voltage source activated after a source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a (4 + 0.3Vx1)A current source, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10C), and 10ohm resistor (10D). The current source, 5ohm resistor, and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 10ohm resistor (10C) and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node B. The current source, 5ohm resistor, and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node C. The current source flows out of node C. Voltage Vx1 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$(4+0.3V_{X1})$~A]				(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, v^=$V_{X1}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the current source into a voltage source. Calculate the value of the transformed source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= [(4 + 0.3V_{X1})~\textrm{A}](5~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V} + 1.5V_{X1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the voltage source activated after the second source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 4 nodes containing a (20 + 1.5Vx1)V voltage source, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10C), and 10ohm resistor (10D). The high-potential end of the voltage source and 5ohm resistor connect at node A. The 5ohm resistor and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node B. The 10ohm resistor (10C) and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node C. The low-potential end of the voltage source and 10ohm resistor (10D) connect at node D. Voltage Vx1 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$(20+1.5V_{X1})$~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, v^=$V_{X1}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_{X1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X1} \amp= (20~\textrm{V} + 1.5V_{X1})\left(\frac{10~\Omega}{5~\Omega+10~\Omega+10~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (20~\textrm{V} + 1.5V_{X1})\left(\frac{10~\Omega}{25~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (20~\textrm{V} + 1.5V_{X1})(0.4)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V} + 0.6V_{X1}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.4V_{X1} \amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_{X1} \amp= 20~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit with only the current source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx2 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx2 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node A. Both current sources flow out of node A. The 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx2 VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node B. The VCCS flows into node B. The 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. The 2A source flows into node C.  Voltage Vx2 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node C to node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.3$V_X2$, *-*]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=2~A]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistors that are in parallel.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit with only the current source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans6">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 0.3Vx2 VCCS, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The 0.3Vx2 VCCS, 5ohm resistor, and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node A. The VCCS flows into node A. The 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node B. The 2A source flows into node B. The VCCS, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. Both current sources flow out of node C. Voltage Vx2 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [cI, l^=0.3$V_{X2}$]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_{X2}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation of the dependent source will be a useful method to solve this circuit, for the same reason that was explained above. (Source transformation on the independent source would not be helpful because it would modify the position of the resistor in parallel with the source while not modifying the placement of <m>V_{X2}</m>.) The source transformed circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated after a source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans7">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 1.5Vx2 VCVS, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The high-potential end of the 1.5Vx2 VCVS and 5ohm resistor connect at node A. The 5ohm resistor and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node B. The 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. The 2A source flows into node C. The low-potential end of the VCVS, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node D. The 2A source flows out of node D. Voltage Vx2 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cV, l_=1.5$V_{X2}$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_{X2}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit with only the current source activated after a source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans8">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 1.5Vx2 VCVS, 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The high-potential end of the 1.5Vx2 VCVS and 15ohm resistor connect at node A. The 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node B. The 2A source flows into node B. The low-potential end of the VCVS, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. The 2A source flows out of node C. Voltage Vx2 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cV, l_=1.5$V_{X2}$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_{X2}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform another source transformation on the dependent source.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated after the second source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans9">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes containing a 0.1Vx2 VCCS, 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The VCCS, 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. The VCCS, 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node B. Both current sources flow out of node B. Voltage Vx2 is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [cI, l^=0.1$V_{X2}$]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=15~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_{X2}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because the two current sources are in parallel, they can be combined into an equivalent source. The two resistors can also be combined in parallel.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit with only the current source activated after the second source transformation.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-circuit34figans10">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes containing a (2+0.1Vx2)A VCCS and 6ohm resistor. The VCCS and 6ohm resistor connect at node A. The VCCS flows into node A. The VCCS and 6ohm resistor connect at node B. The VCCS flows out of node B. Voltage Vx2 is defined as dropping over the 6ohm resistor and drops from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [cI, l^=$(2+0.1V_{X2})$~A]			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$V_{X2}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_{X2}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X2} \amp= [(2 + 0.1V_{X2})~\textrm{A}](6~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V} + 0.6V_{X2}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.4V_{X2} \amp= 12~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_{X2} \amp= 30~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Add the two values to calculate the voltage <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= V_{X1} + V_{X2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V} + 30~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3-superpos-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Use superposition to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit35fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit35fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3-superpos-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit35fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 12mA source, 1k resistor (1A), 1k resistor (1B), 1k resistor (1C), 18V source, 1k resistor (1D), 1k resistor (1E), and 0.4Ix CCCS. The 12mA source, 1k resistor (1A), and 1k resistor (1B) connect at node A. The 12mA source flows into node A. The 1k resistor (1B), 1k resistor (1C), and 1k resistor (1D) connect at node B. The 1k resistor (1C) and high-potential end of the 18V source connect at node C. The 1k resistor (1D), 1k resistor (1E), and CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. The 12mA source, 1k resistor (1A), low-potential end of the 18V source, 1k resistor (1E), and CCCS connect at node E. Both current sources flow out of node E. Current Ix is defined through the 1k resistor (1A) and flows from node A to node E. Voltage Vx is defined over the 1k resistor (1D) and drops from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=12~mA]							(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]								(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=18~V, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, v_=$V_X$]			(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [short]								(10,5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l^=0.4$I_X$]						(10,5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>4.1<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit with only the current source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit35figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 12mA source, 1k resistor (1A), 1k resistor (1B), 1k resistor (1C), 1k resistor (1D), 1k resistor (1E), and 0.4Ix1 CCCS. The 12mA source, 1k resistor (1A), and 1k resistor (1B) connect at node A. The 12mA source flows into node A. The 1k resistor (1B), 1k resistor (1C), and 1k resistor (1D) connect at node B. The 1k resistor (1D), 1k resistor (1E), and CCCS connect at node C. The CCCS flows into node C. The 12mA source, 1k resistor (1A), 1k resistor (1C), 1k resistor (1E), and CCCS connect at node D. Both current sources flow out of node D. Current Ix1 is defined through the 1k resistor (1A) and flows from node A to node D. Voltage Vx1 is defined over the 1k resistor (1D) and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=12~mA]								(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$I_{X1}$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X1}$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]								(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l^=0.4$I_{X1}$]						(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because resistors adjacent to each source contain either a voltage drop or current of interest, source transformation is not a useful tool to solve this circuit. Instead, mesh analysis will be used. The circuit is re-drawn with each mesh current defined. (Note that some component values have been hidden so that the mesh labels can be read.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the current source activated, with each mesh current defined.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit35figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Same figure as previous. counter-clockwise mesh currents are defined. Mesh current 12mA flows through the 12mA source and 1k resistor (1A) through nodes D, A, and D. Mesh current Ia flows through the 1k resistor (1A), 1k resistor (1B), and 1k resistor (1C) through nodes D, A, B, and D. Mesh current Ib flows through the 1k resistor (1C), 1k resistor (1D), and 1k resistor (1E) through nodes D, B, C, and D. Mesh current -0.4Ix1 flows through the 1k resistor (1E) and 0.4Ix1 CCCS through nodes D, C, and D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=12~mA]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, *-*, i^=$I_{X1}$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, *-*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X1}$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, *-*]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI]								(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{12~mA}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (6.25,1.25)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (8.75,1.25)node{--0.4$I_{X1}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All subsequent units are in V, A, and &#8486; and will be omitted from the mesh equations to make them easier to read. The two central meshes have equations derived below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>(I_A - 12) + I_A + (I_A - I_B) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>(I_B - I_A) + I_B + (I_B - (-0.4I_{X1})) \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>An equation must be derived to relate the controlling current to the mesh currents.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_{X1} = 12-I_A</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_{X1}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								3 	\amp -1 	\amp 0	 	\amp 12	\\
								-1	\amp 3		\amp 0.4	\amp 0	\\
								1	\amp 0		\amp 1		\amp 12
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 4.105\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp 0.315\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 7.895
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{X1}</m> is a function of mesh current <m>I_B</m>. Read the middle row to determine that <m>I_B = 0.315~\textrm{mA}</m>. Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_{X1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (0.315~\textrm{mA})(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.315~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit with only the voltage source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the voltage source activated.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit35figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 1k resistor (1A), 1k resistor (1B), 1k resistor (1C), 18V source, 1k resistor (1D), 1k resistor (1E), and 0.4Ix2 CCCS. The 1k resistor (1A) and 1k resistor (1B) connect at node A. The 1k resistor (1B), 1k resistor (1C), and 1k resistor (1D) connect at node B. The 1k resistor (1C) and high-potential end of the 18V source connect at node C. The 1k resistor (1D), 1k resistor (1E), and 0.4Ix2 CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. The 1k resistor (1A), low-potential end of the 18V source, 1k resistor (1E), and CCCS connect at node E. The CCCS flows out of node E. Current Ix2 is defined through 1k resistor (1A) and flows from node A to node E. Voltage Vx2 is defined over the 1k resistor (1D) and drops from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_{X2}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=18~V, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X2}$]		(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]								(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.4$I_{X2}$]					(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>For the same reason as above, mesh analysis will be used to calculate <m>V_{X2}</m>. The circuit is re-drawn below to define the mesh currents. (Note that some component values have been hidden so that the mesh labels can be read.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with only the voltage source activated, with each mesh current defined.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit35figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Same figure as previous. Mesh current Ia flows through the 1k resistor (1A), 1k resistor (1B), 1k resistor (1C), and 18V source through nodes E, A, B, C, and E. Mesh current Ib flows through the 18V source, 1k resistor (1C), 1k resistor (1D), and 1k resistor (1E) through nodes E, C, B, D, and E. Mesh current -0.4Ix2 flows through the 1k resistor (1E) and 0.4Ix2 CCCS through nodes E, D, and E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_{X2}$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=18~V, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, v_=$V_{X2}$]		(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, *-*]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]								(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cI]									(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,2.5)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,2.5)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (6.25,2.5)node{--0.4$I_{X2}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The KVL equations for each mesh are derived below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_A + I_A + (I_A - I_B) + 18 \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>-18 + (I_B - I_A) + I_B + (I_B - (-0.4I_{X2})) \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive a dependent source equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_{X2} = -I_A</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_{X1}</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								3 	\amp -1 	\amp 0	 	\amp -18	\\
								-1	\amp 3		\amp 0.4	\amp 18	\\
								1	\amp 0		\amp 1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp -4.737\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp 3.789\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 4.737
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><m>V_{X2}</m> is a function of mesh current <m>I_B</m>. Read the middle row to determine that <m>I_B = 3.789~\textrm{mA}</m>. Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_{X1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (3.789~\textrm{mA})(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.789~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Add the two values to calculate the voltage <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= V_{X1} + V_{X2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.315~\textrm{V} + 3.789~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.1~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Source Transformation</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch3sourcetransformex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit31fig" /> (from the superposition section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>12.7<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Convert the voltage source into a current source. (Converting the current source into a voltage source would not be useful, as it would modify the position of the 560<nbsp />&#8486; resistor without modifying the position of where <m>V_X</m> is measured.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{13~\textrm{V}}{0.39~k\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 33.33~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with the voltage source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit36figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes containing a 33.33mA source, 390ohm resistor, 560ohm resistor, and 22mA source. All circuit elements are wired in parallel. Both current sources flow into node A and out of node B. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 560ohm resistor and drops from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=33.33~mA]						(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=390~$\Omega$, *-*]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=560~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l_=22~mA]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent source of 55.33<nbsp />mA. The two resistors are also in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent resistance of 229.89<nbsp />&#8486;. The reduced circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit36figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes containing a 55.33mA source and 229.89ohm resistor. The circuit elements are wired in parallel. The current source flows into node A and out of node B. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 229.89ohm resistor from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=55.33~mA]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=229.89~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (55.33~\textrm{mA})(0.2299~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12.7~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3sourcetransformex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit32fig" /> (from the superposition section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p><minus />3.64<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>It is not useful to convert the 20<nbsp />V source into a current source. This is because the two current sources would not be in parallel and would not be able to be reduced to an equivalent source. This is shown in the circuit diagram below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram after converting the voltage source to a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit37figansnothelpful">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 20mA source, 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 24mA source, 200ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. The 200mA source, 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, and 24mA source connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. The 400ohm resistor, 24mA source, and 200ohm resistor connect at node B. The 24mA source flows out of node B. The 200ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node C. The 600ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node D. The 200mA source, 100ohm, and 700ohm resistors connect at node E. The 200mA source flows out of node E. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 700ohm resistor from node E to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=200~mA]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] 							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, -*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [I, l_=24~mA, *-*]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]				(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_X$]	(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
					<p>Instead, convert the 24<nbsp />mA source to a voltage source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S	\amp= (24~\textrm{mA})(0.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9.6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram after converting the current source to a voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit37figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 7 nodes containing a 20V source, 100ohm resistor, 9.6V source, 400ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. All elements are wired in series. The high-potential end of the 20V source and the 100ohm resistor connect at node A. The 100ohm resistor and high-potential end of the 9.6V source connect at node B. The low-potential end of the 9.6V source and the 400ohm resistor connect at node C. The 400ohm and 200ohm resistors connect at node D. The 200ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node E. The 600ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node F. The 700ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node G. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 700ohm resistor and drops from node G to node F.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l^=9.6~V]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]		(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_X$]	(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two voltage sources are in series and can be combined into an equivalent source. (Because the 9.6<nbsp />V source is in the opposite direction as the 20<nbsp />V source, it will be subtracted.) All of the resistors are also in series and can be combined into an equivalent resistance. (The 700<nbsp />&#8486; resistor will not be combined, because the voltage drop over that resistor needs to be calculated.) The reduced circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit37figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes containing a 10.4V source, 1300ohm resistor, and 700ohm resistor. All elements are wired in series. The high-potential end of the 10.4V source and 1300ohm resistor connect at node A. The 1300ohm and 700ohm resistors connect at node B. The 700ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 10.4V source connect at node C. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 700ohm resistor from node C to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10.4~V]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1300~$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=700~$\Omega$, v_=$V_X$]		(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_X</m>. Note that the polarity of <m>V_X</m> opposes the polarity of the voltage source, which is why the answer is negative.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= -10.4~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{700~\Omega}{1300~\Omega+700~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -3.64~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3sourcetransformex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit33fig" /> (from the superposition section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>3.44<nbsp />mA</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>It doesn't make sense to convert the 6<nbsp />mA source into a voltage source, as that voltage source would not be in series with any other voltage source, precluding the possibility of combining sources together to reduce the circuit. This is shown in the circuit diagram below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the 6<nbsp />mA source transformed into a voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit38figansnothelpful2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes containing a 108V source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 24V source, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The high-potential end of the 108V source and 18k resistor are connected at node A. The 18k resistor, 12k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node B. The 12k resistor and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node C. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node D. The 1k and 10k resistors connect at node E. The low-potential end of the 108V source, low-potential end of the 24V source, and 10k resistor connect at node F. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node E to node F.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=108~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=18~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=12~k$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
					<p>Converting the 5<nbsp />V source into a current source would similarly be unhelpful. This is shown in the circuit diagram below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the 5<nbsp />V source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit38figansnothelpful1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 24V source, 1k resistor, 5mA source, and 10k resistor. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 12k resistor, 1k resistor, and 5mA source connect at node A. The 6mA source flows into node A and the 5mA source flows out of node A. The 12k resistor and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node B. The 1k resistor, 10k resistor, and 5mA source connect at node C. The 5mA source flows into node C. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, low-potential end of the 24V source, and 10k resistor connect at node D. The 6mA source flows out of node D. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]							(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]								(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=18~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=12~k$\Omega$, *-]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V, -*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [I, l^=5~mA, -*]						(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,6.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,6.5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]								(5,6.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [short]								(7.5,6.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
					<p>Instead, convert the 24<nbsp />V source into a current source. This transformed current source will be in parallel with the 6<nbsp />mA source, allowing the circuit to be reduced.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{24~\textrm{V}}{12~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the 24<nbsp />V source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit38figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 6mA source, 18k resistor, 2mA source, 12k resistor, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 2mA source, 12k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node A. The high-potential end of the 5V source and the 1k resistor connect at node B. The 1k and 10k resistors connect at node C. The 6mA source, 18k resistor, 2mA source, 12k resistor, and 10k resistor connect at node D. Both current sources flow out of node D. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=18~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=2~mA, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(12.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]	(12.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(12.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent source of 8<nbsp />mA. Furthermore, the 18<nbsp />k&#8486; and 12<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent resistance of 7.2<nbsp />k&#8486;. The reduced circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram with the 24<nbsp />V source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit38figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing an 8mA source, 7.2k resistor, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. The 8mA source, 7.2k resistor, and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node A. The 8mA source flows into node A. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node B. The 1k and 10k resistors connect at node C. The 8mA source, 7.2k resistor, and 10k resistor connect at node D. The 8mA source flows out of node D. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=8~mA]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=7.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>It would still not be helpful to convert the 5<nbsp />V source into a current source. Instead, convert the 8<nbsp />mA current source into a voltage source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= (8~\textrm{mA})(7.2~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 57.6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the 8<nbsp />mA source transformed into a voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit38figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 5 nodes containing a 57.6V source. 7.2k resistor, 5V source, 1k resistor, and 10k resistor. All circuit elements are wired in series. The high-potential end of the 57.6V source and 7.2k resistor connect at node A. The 7.2k resistor and low-potential end of the 5V source connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 5V source and 1k resistor connect at node C. The 1k resistor and 10k resistor connect at node D. The 10k resistor and low-potential end of the 57.6V source connect at node E. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 10k resistor from node D to node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=57.6~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=7.2~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two voltage sources are in series and can be combined into an equivalent voltage source. The three resistors are in series and can be combined into a single equivalent resistance.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit38figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes containing a 62.6V source and 18.2k resistor. The high-potential end of the 62.6V source and 18.2k resistor connect at node A. The low-potential end of the 62.6V source and 18.2k resistor connect at node B. Current Ix flows through the 18.2k resistor from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=62.6~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=18.2~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= \frac{62.6~\textrm{V}}{18.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.44~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3sourcetransformex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit34fig" /> (from the superposition section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>50<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Note that while source transformation was used in the solution in the superposition section, this method will use source transformation <em>without</em> superposition to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>Convert the 40<nbsp />V source to a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{40~\textrm{V}}{10~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the 40<nbsp />V source converted into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit39figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 4A source, 10ohm resistor (10A), 0.3Vx VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The 4A source, 10ohm resistor (10A), VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node A. The 4A source and VCCS flow into node A. The 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D) and 2A source connect at node B. The 2A source flows into node B. The 4A source, 10ohm resistor (10A), VCCS, 10ohm resistor (10B), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. All current sources flow out of node C. Voltage Vx is defined over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=4~A]							(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.3$V_X$, *-*]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=2~A]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 4<nbsp />A source is in parallel with the <m>0.3V_X</m> source and can be combined into an equivalent source. Two of the 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent resistance of 5<nbsp />&#8486;.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch3-circuit39figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes containing a (4+0.3Vx)A current source, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The (4+0.3Vx) source, 5ohm resistor, and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node B. The 2A source flows into node B. The (4+0.3Vx) source, 5ohm, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. Both current sources flow out of node C. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$(4 + 0.3V_X)$~A]				(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the <m>4 + 0.3V_X~\textrm{A}</m> source into a voltage source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= (4~\textrm{A}+(0.3~\textrm{A/V})V_X)(5~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V} + 1.5V_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the current source transformed into a voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch3-circuit39figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing a (20+1.5Vx)V source, 5ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 5ohm resistor connect at node A. The 5ohm resistor and 10ohm resistor (10C) connect at node B. The 10ohm resistor (10C), 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. The 2A source flows into node C. The low-potential end of the voltage source, the 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node D. The 2A source flows out of node D. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$(20 + 1.5V_X)$~V]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two resistors in series and re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram with the current source transformed into a voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-circuit39figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing a (20+1.5Vx)V source, 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 15ohm resistor connect at node A. The 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node B. The 2A source flows into node B. The low-potential end of the voltage source, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source connect at node C. The 2A source flows out of node C. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) and drops from node B to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$(20 + 1.5V_X)$~V]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}+1.5V_X}{15~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.33~\textrm{A} + (0.1~\textrm{A/V})V_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the voltage source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch3-circuit39figans5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes containing a (1.33+0.1Vx)A source, 15ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor (10D), and 2A source. All circuit elements are wired in parallel with each other. Both current sources flow into node A and out of node B. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 10ohm resistor (10D) from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=$(1.33 + 0.1V_X)$~A]			(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=15~$\Omega$, *-*]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=2~A]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent source. The two resistors are in parallel and can be combined into an equivalent resistance. The reduced circuit diagram is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-circuit39figans6">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes containing a (3.33+0.1Vx)A source and 6ohm resistor. The two circuit elements are wired in parallel. The current source flows into node A and out of node B. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 6ohm resistor from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$(3.33 + 0.1V_X)$~A]			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (3.33~\textrm{A}+(0.1~\textrm{A/V})V_X)(6~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V}+0.6V_X</mrow>
							<mrow>0.4V_X \amp= 20~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{0.4}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3sourcetransformex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Use source transformation to calculate <m>V_X</m> and <m>V_Y</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit310fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit310fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3sourcetransformex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit310fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 2Vx VCVS, 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 0.5A source, 3Vy VCVS, 40ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 2Vx VCVS and 20ohm resistor connect at node A. The 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 0.5A source, and low-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS connect at node B. The 0.5A source flows into node B. The high-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS and 40ohm resistor connect at node C. The 40ohm and 50ohm resistors connect at node D. The low-potential end of the 2Vx VCVS, 10ohm resistor, 0.5A source, and 50ohm resistor connect at node E. The 0.5A source flows out of node E. Voltage Vy is defined as dropping over the 40ohm resistor from node C to node D. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 50ohm resistor from node D to node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cV, l_=2$V_X$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=0.5~A, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cV, l_=3$V_Y$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$, v_=$V_Y$]		(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= -2.91~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_Y \amp= -2.35~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>It does not make sense to transform the <m>3V_Y</m> source because it would modify the placement of the resistor over which <m>V_Y</m> will be dropped without changing the position at which <m>V_Y</m> is measured.</p>

					<p>It does not make sense to transform the current source, as the voltage source would not be in series with any other source, leading to a circuit that cannot be reduced. This is shown in the circuit diagram below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the current source transformed into a voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit310fignothelpful">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes containing a 2Vx VCVS, 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 5V source, 3Vy VCVS, 40ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 2Vx VCVS and 20ohm resistor connect at node A. The 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, and low-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS and 40ohm resistor connect at node C. The 40ohm and 50ohm resistors connect at node D. The 10ohm resistor and high-potential end of the 5V source connect at node E. The low-potential end of the 2Vx VCVS, low-potential end of the 5V source, and 50ohm resistor connect at node F. Voltage Vy is defined as dropping over the 40ohm resistor from node C to node D. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 50ohm resistor from node D to node F.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [cV, l_=2$V_X$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]					(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V, -*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [cV, l_=3$V_Y$]						(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$, v_=$V_Y$]			(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Instead, convert the <m>2V_X</m> dependent source into a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{2V_X}{20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.1V_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit diagram.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the <m>2V_X</m> source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit310figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 0.1Vx VCCS, 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 0.5A source, 3Vy VCVS, 40ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. The 0.1Vx VCCS, 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 0.5A source, and low-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS connect at node A. Both current sources flow into node A. The high-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS and 40ohm resistor connect at node B. The 40ohm and 50ohm resistors connect at node C. The 0.1Vx VCCS, 20ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, 0.5A source, and 50ohm resistor connect at node D. Voltage Vy is defined as dropping over the 40ohm resistor between node B and node C. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 50ohm resistor between node C and node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.1$V_X$]					(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=0.5~A, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cV, l_=3$V_Y$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$, v_=$V_Y$]		(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(10,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources are in parallel and can be combined into a single equivalent source. The 10<nbsp />&#8486; and 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined into a single equivalent resistance. Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram with the <m>2V_X</m> source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch3-circuit310figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing a (0.5A+0.1Vx) current source, 6.67ohm resistor, 3Vy VCVS, 40ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. The current source, 6.67ohm resistor, and low-potential end of the VCVS connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The high-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS and 40ohm resistor connect at node B. The 40ohm and 50ohm resistors connect at node C. The current source, 6.67ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor connect at node D. The current source flows out of node D. Voltage Vy is defined as dropping over the 40ohm resistor from node B to node C. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 50ohm resistor between node C and node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=0.5~A + 0.1$V_X$]				(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6.67~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l_=3$V_Y$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$, v_=$V_Y$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the current source into a voltage source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= (0.5~\textrm{A}+(0.1~\textrm{A/V})V_X)(6.67~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.33~\textrm{V} + 0.67V_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the current source transformed into a voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch3-circuit310figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 5 nodes containing a (3.33V + 0.067Vx) voltage source, 6.67ohm resistor, 3Vy VCVS, 40ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. All circuit elements are connected in series. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 6.67ohm resistor connect at node A. The 6.67ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 3Vy VCVS and 40ohm resistor connect at node C. The 40ohm and 50ohm resistors connect at node D. The low-potential end of the voltage source and 50ohm resistor connect at node E. Voltage Vy is defined as dropping over the 40ohm resistor from node C to node D. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 50ohm resistor from node D to node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=3.33~V + 0.67$V_X$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6.67~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l_=3$V_Y$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$, v_=$V_Y$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two voltage sources are in series and can be combined. While the resistors are also all in series, it doesn't make sense to combine them into an equivalent resistance because the voltage drops over two of the three resistors need to be calculated. Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-circuit310figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 4 nodes containing a (3.33V + 0.67Vx + 3Vy) voltage source, 6.67ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor. All circuit elements are connected in series. The high-potential end of the source and the 6.67ohm resistor connect at node A. The 6.67ohm and 40ohm resistors connect at node B. The 40ohm and 50ohm resistors connect at node C. The low-potential end of the voltage source and 50ohm resistor connect at node D. Voltage Vy is defined as dropping over the 40ohm resistor from node B to node C. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 50ohm resistor from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=3.33~V + 0.67$V_X$ + 3$V_Y$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6.67~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$, v_=$V_Y$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to derive equations for <m>V_X</m> and <m>V_Y</m>. (All units are in V, A, and <nbsp />&#8486; and are omitted from the equations for readability.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (3.33 + 0.67V_X + 3V_Y)\left(\frac{40}{96.67}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{96.67}{40}V_X \amp= 3.33 + 0.67V_X + 3V_Y</mrow>
							<mrow>\left(\frac{96.67}{40} - 0.67\right)V_X - 3V_Y \amp= 3.33</mrow>
							<mrow>1.26V_X - 3V_Y \amp= 3.33</mrow>
							<mrow>V_Y \amp= (3.33 + 0.67V_X + 3V_Y)\left(\frac{50}{96.67}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{96.67}{50}V_Y \amp= 3.33 + 0.67V_X + 3V_Y</mrow>
							<mrow>\left(\frac{96.67}{50}-3\right)V_Y - 0.67V_X \amp= 3.33</mrow>
							<mrow>-0.67V_X - 0.583V_Y \amp= 3.3</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>There are two equations and two unknowns. They will be placed into a matrix to solve for each quantity. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>V_Y</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.26	\amp -3		\amp 3.33	\\
								-0.67	\amp -0.583	\amp 3.33
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp -2.94\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp -2.35
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Read the top row of the matrix to find the value of <m>V_X</m> and the bottom row to find the value of <m>V_Y</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= -2.94~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_Y \amp= -2.35~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Th&#233;venin and Norton's Theorems</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch3theveninex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit311fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit311fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3theveninex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit311fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 4 nodes containing a 10V source, 60ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, and 30ohm resistor. All circuit elements are connected in series. The low-potential end of the 10V source and 30ohm resistor connect at node A. The 30ohm and 10ohm resistors connect at node B. The high-potential end of the 10V source and 60ohm resistor connect at node C. The 10ohm and 60ohm resistors connect at node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=60~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};	
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure xml:id="circuit311figans">
  						<caption>Answer to <xref ref="ch3theveninex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit311figans">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 0 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 3V source and 21ohm resistor. All circuit elements are connected in series. One end of the 21ohm resistor connects to node A. The other end of the 21ohm resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 3V source at node C. The high-potential end of the 3V source connects at node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [V, l^=3~V]				(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=21~$\Omega$, -o]	(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]				(2.5,0) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage (which is the voltage dropped between nodes a and b). Note that the three resistors are in series with a voltage source, which means that the voltage divider rule can be used to calculate this voltage. Note also that polarity of the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage opposes the polarity of the voltage source, so the sign of the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage will be negative. (This is why the voltage source in <xref ref="circuit311figans" /> appears <q>upside-down.</q>)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{TH}	\amp=	-10~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{30~\Omega}{60~\Omega + 10~\Omega + 30~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	-10~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{30~\Omega}{100~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	-3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>

					<p>Second, calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance. Deactivate the voltage source and calculate the equivalent resistance as seen between nodes a and b. The circuit diagram with the voltage source deactivated is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit311figans1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the voltage source deactivated.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch3-circuit311figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 60ohm resistor, 10ohm resistor, and 30ohm resistor. All circuit elements are connected in series. The 30ohm and 60ohm resistors connect at node A. The 30ohm and 10ohm resistors connect at node B. The 60ohm and 10ohm resistors connect at node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=60~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};	
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the 60<nbsp />&#8486; resistor in series with the 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and re-draw the circuit diagram.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit311figans2">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the voltage source deactivated and the 10<nbsp />&#8486; and 60<nbsp />&#8486; resistors combined in series.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch3-circuit311figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 30ohm resistor and 70ohm resistor. The 70ohm resistor and 30ohm resistor connect at node A. The 70ohm resistor and 30ohm resistor connect at node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=70~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};	
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance is equal to the parallel combination of the 30<nbsp />&#8486; and 70<nbsp />&#8486; resistors.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH}	\amp=	30~\Omega//70~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	21~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3theveninex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Norton equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit312fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit312fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3theveninex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 3V source, 1.5k resistor, 2.2k resistor, and 26mA source. The 1.5k resistor, 2.2k resistor, and 26mA source connect at node A. The 26mA source flows into node A. The low-potential end of the 3V source, 2.2k resistor, and 26mA source connect at node B. The 26mA source flows out of node B. The high-potential end of the 3V source and 1.5k resistor connect at node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1.5~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=26~mA, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure xml:id="circuit312figans">
  						<caption>Answer to <xref ref="ch3theveninex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312figans">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit containing 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 28mA source and 892ohm resistor. Both elements are wired in parallel between nodes A and B. The 28mA source flows out of node B and into node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=28~mA]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=892~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]				(3.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]				(3.5,0) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>There are two techniques to solve this circuit. Source transformation is the most direct means of solving the circuit and will be used first. Superposition can also be used, and this process will be described after source transformation is used.</p>

					<p>If using source transformation, it's most beneficial to transform voltage sources into current sources (if possible), as this will most easily lead to a Norton equivalent circuit. To that end, first transform the voltage source into a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S	\amp=	\frac{3~\textrm{V}}{1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>		\amp=	2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit312figans1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the voltage source transformed into a current source.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit containing 3 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 2mA source, 1.5k resistor, 2.2k resistor, and 26mA source. All circuit elements are wired in parallel between nodes A and B. Both current sources flow out of node B and into node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=2~mA]						(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=26~mA, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources are in parallel and can be added, this also corresponds to the Norton equivalent current of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_N \amp= 2~\textrm{mA} + 26~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 28~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two resistors can be combined in parallel, this also corresponds to the Norton equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_N \amp= 1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega // 2.2~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.892~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 892~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>To use superposition to derive the Norton equivalent circuit, first analyze the circuit with the current source deactivated. This circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit312figans2">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the current source deactivated.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit containing 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 3V source, 1.5k resistor, and 2.2k resistor. All circuit elements are connected in series. The 1.5k and 2.2k resistors connect at node A. The 2.2k resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 3V source at node B. The high-potential end of the 3V source and 1.5k resistor connect at node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1.5~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The Norton equivalent current through this subcircuit is equal to the current through nodes a and b when those two nodes are shorted. Shorting those nodes effectively removes the 2.2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor from the circuit. This is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit312figans3">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the current source deactivated and a short placed between nodes a and b.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 3V source and 1.5k resistor. Both circuit elements are wired in series. The high-potential end of the 3V source connects to the 1.5k resistor at node C. The 1.5k resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 3V source at node A/B (A is shorted to B). Current In1 is defined as flowing through the 1.5k resistor from node C to node A/B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1.5~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [short, *-*, i^=$I_{N1}$]	(3,0);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the current <m>I_{N1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{N1} \amp= \frac{3~\textrm{V}}{1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Next, analyze the circuit with the voltage source deactivated. This circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit312figans4">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the voltage source deactivated.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 1.5k resistor, 2.2k resistor, and 26mA source. All circuit elements are wired in parallel between nodes A and B. The 26mA source flows from node B to node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1.5~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=26~mA, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};	
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The Norton equivalent current through this subcircuit is equal to the current through nodes a and b when those two nodes are shorted. Shorting those nodes effectively removes both resistors from the circuit. (This is because both resistors are in parallel with the short.) This is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit312figans5">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the voltage source deactivated and a short placed between nodes a and b.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312figans5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 1 node consisting of a 26mA source. Both ends of the 26mA source are shorted together, representing node A/B. Current In2 is defined as flowing in the same direction as the 26mA source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=26~mA]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:2.5) node[right] {b};	
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, *-*, i^=$I_{N2}$]	(7.5,0);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The quantity <m>I_{N2}</m> is equal to the source current, 26<nbsp />mA.</p>

					<p>The total Norton equivalent current can now be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_N \amp= I_{N1} + I_{N2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{mA} + 26~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 28~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>To calculate the Norton equivalent resistance, deactivate both sources and calculate the equivalent resistance as seen through nodes a and b. The circuit with both independent sources deactivated is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit312figans6">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the both sources deactivated.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit312figans6">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 1.5k resistor and 2.2k resistor. The 1.5k and 2.2k resistors connect at node A. The 1.5k and 2.2k resistors connect at node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1.5~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The Norton equivalent resistance is equal to the parallel combination of both resistors.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_N \amp= 1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega // 2.2~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.892~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 892~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3theveninex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit313fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit313fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3theveninex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit313fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 6V source, 3k resistor, 5k resistor, 1k resistor, and 4Ix CCCS. The 3k and 5k resistors connect at node A. The 1k resistor and 4Ix CCCS connect at node B. The CCCS flows out of node B. The high-potential end of the 6V source and 3k resistor connect at node C. The low-potential end of the 6V source, 5k resistor, 1k resistor, and 4Ix CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 3k resistor from node C to node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=4$I_X$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (left:0.5) node[above] {b};			
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure xml:id="circuit313figans">
  						<caption>Answer to <xref ref="ch3theveninex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit313figans">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 0 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 6.75V source and 375ohm resistor. One end of the 375ohm resistor connects to node A. The other end of the 375ohm resistor and the high-potential end of the 6.75V source connect at node C. The low-potential end of the 6.75V source connects at node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6.75~V]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=375~$\Omega$, -o]	(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]				(2.5,0) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, calculate the open-circuit voltage between nodes a and b. This solution method treats the left and right circuits as independent circuits. The left circuit will be used to calculate <m>V_a</m>, the right circuit will be used to calculate <m>V_b</m>, and the relationship <m>V_{ab} = V_a - V_b</m> will be used to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage. (Why not use mesh analysis? Solving for the mesh currents is not terribly useful in this case as the voltage divider rule will need to be used on the left side of the circuit regardless, and the current through the right side of the circuit can be calculated relatively easily without having to use mesh analysis.)</p>

					<p>The circuit is drawn below with the quantities <m>V_a</m> and <m>V_b</m> explicitly labeled.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit313figans1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with <m>V_a</m> and <m>V_b</m> explicitly labeled.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit313figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit is identical to figure in the question text. Voltage Va is defined as dropping over the 5k resistor from node A to node D. Voltage Vb is defined as dropping over the 1k resistor from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_a$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_b$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=4$I_X$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (left:0.5) node[above] {b};			
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_a</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_a \amp= 6~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega+5~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.75~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The current <m>I_X</m> needs to be known to solve the right side of the circuit. Use Ohm's law to calculate this value.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= \frac{6~\textrm{V}}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega+5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.75~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now analyze the right side of the circuit. Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_b</m>. Note that the sign is negative as the current flows from low to high potential as far as <m>V_b</m> is defined.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_b \amp= -4I_X(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -(4)(0.75~\textrm{mA})(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage can now be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{TH} \amp= V_{ab} = V_a - V_b</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.75~\textrm{V} - (-3~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.75~\textrm{V} + 3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.75~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because this circuit contains a dependent source, the short-circuit current needs to be calculated to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance. This circuit contains only one independent source, which means that superposition cannot be used. There are no reductions in the circuit that can be done without eliminating important quantities we need to know. (We cannot combine the two resistors in parallel without eliminating the branch through which we need to measure <m>I_{SC}</m>.) Mesh analysis is therefore the easiest method to use to solve for <m>I_{SC}</m>. The circuit is drawn below. (Note: some of the component labels have been hidden so that the mesh current labels can be read.)</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit313figans2">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with nodes a and b shorted, and with mesh currents labeled.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit313figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 6V source, 3k resistor, 5k resistor, 1k resistor, and 4Ix CCCS. The high-potential end of the 6V source and 3k resistor connect at node C. The 3k resistor, 5k resistor,1k resistor, and CCCS connect at node A/B (nodes A and B are shorted). The CCCS flows out of node A/B. The low-potential end of the 6V source, 5k resistor, 1k resistor, and 4Ix CCCS connect at node D. The CCCS flows into node D. Mesh current Ia flows through the 6V source, 3k resistor, and 5k resistor through nodes D, C, A/B, and D. Mesh current Ib flows through the 5k and 1k resistors through nodes D, A/B, and D. Mesh current Ic flows through the 1k resistor and CCCS through nodes D, A/B, and D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$]		(2.5,2.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, *-*]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$]				(5,2.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cI, l^=4$I_X$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (6.25,1.25)node{$I_C$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All mesh equations will be expressed with units of V, mA, and k&#8486; and will be omitted from each equation to make them easier to read. First, use KVL around the left mesh.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-6 + 3I_A + 5(I_A - I_B) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Next, use KVL around the center mesh.</p>

					<p>
						<md>5(I_B - I_A) + 1(I_B - I_C) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The right mesh contains a current source that is not shared with any other mesh. Mesh current <m>I_C</m> has the same direction as the source current. Therefore, <m>I_C = 4I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>Finally, use the left mesh to relate the controlling current <m>I_X</m> with branch current <m>I_A</m>. These two values are equal in both magnitude and direction: <m>I_X = I_A</m>.</p>

					<p>The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_C</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								8 	\amp -5 	\amp 0 		\amp 0	\amp 6\\
								-5 	\amp 6		\amp -1		\amp 0	\amp 0\\
								0	\amp 0		\amp 1		\amp -4	\amp 0\\
								1	\amp 0		\amp 0		\amp -1	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0	\amp 0 		\amp 12\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0	\amp 0		\amp 18\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 0		\amp 48\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 12
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the branch current <m>I_{SC}</m> is contained within a single mesh, the short-circuit current is equal to that mesh current. In other words, <m>I_{SC} = I_B</m>. Therefore, <m>I_{SC}</m> is equal to 18<nbsp />mA.</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{TH}}{I_{SC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{6.75~\textrm{V}}{18~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.375~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 375~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3theveninex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit314fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit314fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3theveninex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit314fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 2V source, 200ohm resistor (200A), 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 4Ix CCCS, 800ohm resistor, and 200ohm (200B) resistor. The high-potential end of the 2V source and 200ohm resistor (200A) connect at node C. The 200ohm (200A), 100ohm, and 400ohm resistors connect at node D. The 400ohm resistor, 4Ix CCCS, and 800ohm resistor connect at node E. The CCCS flows into node E. The 800ohm resistor, 4Ix CCCS, and 200ohm resistor (200B) connect at node A. The CCCS flows out of node A. The low-potential end of the 2V source, 100ohm resistor, and 200ohm resistor (200B) connect at node B. Ix is defined as flowing through the 100ohm resistor from node D to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=2~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=4$I_X$, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=800~$\Omega$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};				
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure xml:id="circuit314figans">
  						<caption>Answer to <xref ref="ch3theveninex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit314figans">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 0 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 6.2V source and 260ohm resistor. One end of the 260ohm resistor connects to node A. The other end of the 260ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 6.2V source at node C. The low-potential end of the 6.2V source connects to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6.2~V]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=260~$\Omega$, -o]	(2.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]				(2.5,0) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Mesh analysis is the easiest way to solve this circuit. Superposition cannot be used as there is only one independent source. Source transformation cannot be used on the independent source without eliminating the branch through which <m>I_X</m> needs to be measured. However, source transformation can be used on the dependent source and will reduce the number of meshes from three to two. Convert the dependent current source to a dependent voltage source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_D \amp= (800~\Omega)(4I_X)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (3200~\textrm{V/A})I_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit is re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit314figans1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with the dependent current source converted to a dependent voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit314figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes consisting of a 2V source, 200ohm resistor (200A), 100ohm resistor, 400ohm resistor, 800ohm resistor, (3200A/V)Ix CCVS, and 200ohm resistor (200B). The high-potential end of the 2V source is connected to the 200ohm resistor (200A) at node C. The 200ohm resistor (200A), 100ohm resistor, and 400ohm resistor connect at node D. The 400ohm and 800ohm resistors connect at node E. The 800ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node F. The 200ohm resistor (200B) connects to the low-potential end of the CCVS at node A. The low-potential end of the 2V source, 100ohm resistor, and 200ohm resistor (200B) connect at node B. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 100ohm resistor from node D to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=2~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=400~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cV, l_=(3200~A/V)$I_X$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=800~$\Omega$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};				
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 400<nbsp />&#8486; resistor can be combined in series with the 800<nbsp />&#8486; resistor. The reduced circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit314figans2">
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram with the dependent current source converted to a dependent voltage source.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit314figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 2V source, 200ohm resistor (200A), 100ohm resistor, 1200ohm resistor, (3200A/V)Ix CCVS, and 200ohm resistor (200B). The high-potential end of the 2V source and 200ohm resistor (200A) connect at node C. The 200ohm (200A), 100ohm, and 1200ohm resistors connect at node D. The 1200ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node E. The low-potential end of the CCVS and 200ohm resistor (200B) connect at node A. The low-potential end of the 2V source, 100ohm resistor, and 200ohm resistor (200B) connect at node B. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 100ohm resistor from node D to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=2~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1200~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l^=(3200~A/V)$I_X$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$, -*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};				
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit is depicted below with the two mesh currents defined. Note that some component labels may be hidden to make the mesh current labels easier to read.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit314figans3">
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram with the dependent current source converted to a dependent voltage source with mesh currents defined.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit314figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit diagram as preceding diagram. Mesh current Ia flows through the 2V source, 200ohm resistor (200A) and 100ohm resistor through nodes B, C, D, and B. Mesh current Ib flows through the 100ohm resistor, 1200ohm resistor, CCVS, and 200ohm resistor (200B) through nodes B, D, E, A, and B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=2~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, *-*, i_=$I_X$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1200~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l^=(3200~A/V)$I_X$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$, -*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};	
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);			
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All mesh equations will use V, A, and &#8486; to make the equations more readable. Use KCL around the left mesh to derive the first equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-2 + 200I_A + 100(I_A-I_B) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL around the right mesh to derive the second equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>100(I_B - I_A) + 1200I_B + 3200I_X + 200I_B = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive an equation relating the controlling current to the mesh currents.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_X = I_A - I_B</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								300 	\amp -100 	\amp 0 		\amp 2\\
								-100 	\amp 1500 	\amp 3200	\amp 0\\
								-1		\amp 1		\amp 1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.017\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp 0.031\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp -0.014
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage is equal to the voltage dropped over the 200<nbsp />&#8486; resistor. Use Ohm's law to calculate this voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{TH} \amp= (200~\Omega)(I_B)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (200~\Omega)(0.031~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Short nodes a and b to calculate <m>I_{SC}</m>. This is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit314figans4">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram used to calculate <m>I_{SC}</m>.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch3-circuit314figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 2V source, 200ohm resistor (200A), 100ohm resistor, 1200ohm resistor, and (3200 A/V)Ix CCVS. The high-potential end of the 2V source and 200ohm resistor (200A) connect at node C. The 200ohm (200A), 100ohm, and 1200ohm resistors connect at node D. The 1200ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node E. The low-potential end of both voltage sources connect to the 100ohm resistor at node A/B (nodes A and B are shorted). Ix is defined as flowing through the 100ohm resistor from node D to node A/B. Isc is defined as flowing through the A/B short in the direction from the low-potential end of the CCVS to the connection between the 100ohm resistor and low-potential end of the 2V source. Mesh current Ia flows through the 2V source, 200ohm resistor (200A), and 100ohm resistor through nodes A/B, C, D, and A/B. Mesh current Ib flows through the 100ohm resistor, 1200ohm resistor, and CCVS through nodes A/B, D, E, and A/B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=2~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, *-*, i_=$I_X$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1200~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l^=(3200~A/V)$I_X$]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-, i_=$I_{SC}$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (down:0.5) node[below] {b};	
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All mesh equations will use V, A, and &#8486; to make the equations more readable. Use KCL around the left mesh to derive the first equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-2 + 200I_A + 100(I_A-I_B) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL around the right mesh to derive the second equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>100(I_B - I_A) + 1200I_B + 3200I_X = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive an equation relating the controlling current to the mesh currents.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_X = I_A - I_B</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								300 	\amp -100 	\amp 0 		\amp 2\\
								-100 	\amp 1300 	\amp 3200	\amp 0\\
								-1		\amp 1		\amp 1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.015\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp 0.024\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp -0.009
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The short-circuit current is equal to mesh current <m>I_B</m>. Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{TH}}{I_{SC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{6.2~\textrm{V}}{0.024~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 260~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch3theveninex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive the Norton equivalent circuit between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit315fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit315fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch3theveninex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit315fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 20V source, 50ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, 10Vx VVVS, 0.5Ix CCCS, and 100ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source and 50ohm resistor connect at node C. The 50ohm and 200ohm resistors connect at node D. The 200ohm resistor and high-potential end of the 10Vx VCVS connect at node E. The 0.5Ix CCCS and 100ohm resistor connect at node A. The CCCS flows into node A. The low-potential end of both voltage sources, the 0.5Ix CCCS, and 100ohm resistor connect at node B. The CCCS flows out of node B. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 50ohm resistor from node C to node D. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 100ohm resistor from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=50~$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l_=10$V_X$, -*]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cI, l^=0.5$I_X$, *-]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};				
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure xml:id="circuit315figans">
  						<caption>Answer to <xref ref="ch3theveninex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch3-circuit315figans">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 0.04A source and 33ohm resistor. Both circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes A and B. The current source flows out of node B and into node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=0.04~A]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=33.3~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]					(3.5,2.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]				(3.5,0) node[right] {b};		
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, reduce the circuit as much as possible. The two resistors in the left mesh of the circuit are in series and can be combined. The two voltage sources are also in series and can be combined. The reduced circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit315figans1">
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch3-circuit315figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a (20V - 10Vx) voltage source, 250ohm resistor, 0.5Ix CCCS, and 100ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the voltage source and 250ohm resistor connect at node C. The 0.5Ix CCCS and 100ohm resistor connect at node A. The CCCS flows into node A. The low-potential end of the voltage source, 0.5Ix CCCS, and 100ohm resistor connect at node B. The CCCS flows out of node B. Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 250ohm resistor from node C to node B. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 100ohm resistor from node A to node B.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V -- 10$V_X$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=250~$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=0.5$I_X$, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};			
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit contains a dependent source. Therefore, the open-circuit voltage will be calculated along with the Norton equivalent current to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance. First, use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}-10V_X}{250~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.08~\textrm{A} - (0.04~\textrm{A/V})V_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>, which is the open-circuit voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (100~\Omega)(0.5I_X)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (100~\Omega)(0.5)(0.08~\textrm{A} - (0.04~\textrm{A/V})V_X)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V} - 2V_X</mrow>
							<mrow>3V_X \amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= 1.33~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Short the nodes between a and b and re-draw the circuit. <m>V_X</m> is now equal to zero. This is because <m>V_X</m> is measured over the 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, which is now shorted out, and by definition has a voltage drop of zero. This causes the VCVS in the left mesh to contribute no voltage to the circuit. The reduced circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit315figans2">
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram with nodes a and b shorted.</caption>
  						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch3-circuit315figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 20V source, 250ohm resistor, and 0.5Ix CCCS. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to the 250ohm resistor at node C. The 250ohm resistor, low-potential end of the voltage source, and 0.5Ix current source flow into node A/B (nodes A and B are shorted). Current Ix is defined as flowing through the 250ohm resistor from node C to node A/B. Current In is defined as flowing from nodes A to B (which are now shorted). The CCCS flows out of node B into node A (which are shorted).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=250~$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=0.5$I_X$, *-]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, *-*, i^=$I_N$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {a};
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [short, -o] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {b};			
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X	\amp=	\frac{20~\textrm{V}}{250~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> 		\amp=	0.08~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL to calculate <m>I_N</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_N	\amp=	0.5I_X</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp=	0.5(0.08~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp=	0.04~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Norton equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_N	\amp=	\frac{1.33~\textrm{V}}{0.04~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp=	33.3~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>

			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Maximum Power Transfer</title>
		
			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the resistance for maximum power transfer and the maximum amount of power transferred to the load under that condition for the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit311fig" /> (from the Th&#233;venin and Norton's theorem section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 21~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= 0.107~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p><m>R_{LOAD}</m> will be equal to the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance of 21<nbsp />&#8486;. (This value is derived in <xref ref="ch3theveninex1" />. Refer to that solution for details.) Use <xref ref="theMaximumPower" /> to calculate the maximum power transferred to the load.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{MAX}	\amp=	\frac{V_{TH}^2}{4R_{TH}}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	\frac{(-3~\textrm{V})^2}{4(21~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	0.107~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the resistance for maximum power transfer, and the maximum amount of power transferred to the load under that condition, for the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit312fig" /> (from the Th&#233;venin and Norton's theorem section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 892~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= 0.175~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p><m>R_{LOAD}</m> will be equal to the Norton equivalent resistance of 892<nbsp />&#8486;. (This value is derived in <xref ref="ch3theveninex2" />. Refer to that solution for details.) Use <xref ref="theMaximumPowerNorton" /> to calculate the maximum power transferred to the load.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{MAX}	\amp=	\frac{I_N^2R_N}{4}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	\frac{(0.028~\textrm{A})^2(892~\Omega)}{4}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	0.175~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the resistance for maximum power transfer, and the maximum amount of power transferred to the load under that condition, for the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit313fig" /> (from the Th&#233;venin and Norton's theorem section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 375~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= 0.030~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p><m>R_{LOAD}</m> will be equal to the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance of 375<nbsp />&#8486;. (This value is derived in <xref ref="ch3theveninex3" />. Refer to that solution for details.) Use <xref ref="theMaximumPower" /> to calculate the maximum power transferred to the load.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{MAX}	\amp=	\frac{V_{TH}^2}{4R_{TH}}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	\frac{(6.75~\textrm{V})^2}{4(375~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	0.03~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the resistance for maximum power transfer, and the maximum amount of power transferred to the load under that condition, for the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit314fig" /> (from the Th&#233;venin and Norton's theorem section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 260~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= 0.37~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p><m>R_{LOAD}</m> will be equal to the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance of 260<nbsp />&#8486;. (This value is derived in <xref ref="ch3theveninex4" />. Refer to that solution for details.) Use <xref ref="theMaximumPower" /> to calculate the maximum power transferred to the load.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{MAX}	\amp=	\frac{V_{TH}^2}{4R_{TH}}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	\frac{(6.2~\textrm{V})^2}{4(260~\Omega)}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	0.37~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the resistance for maximum power transfer, and the maximum amount of power transferred to the load under that condition, for the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit315fig" /> (from the Th&#233;venin and Norton's theorem section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD} \amp= 33~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>P_{MAX} \amp= 0.013~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p><m>R_{LOAD}</m> will be equal to the Norton equivalent resistance of 33<nbsp />&#8486;. (This value is derived in <xref ref="ch3theveninex5" />. Refer to that solution for details.) Use <xref ref="theMaximumPowerNorton" /> to calculate the maximum power transferred to the load.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P_{MAX}	\amp=	\frac{I_N^2R_N}{4}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	\frac{(0.04~\textrm{A})^2(33~\Omega)}{4}</mrow>
							<mrow>			\amp=	0.013~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>
	</exercises>
</chapter>